// v3316 -- A DIAGNOSTIC QUESTION THAT FOUND A REAL BUG IN THE ANSWER RATHER THAN IN THE THING ASKED ABOUT.
//
// ASKED: could the v3284 peer-trust work be causing server.html's timeout collection, on a mostly-wireless LAN
// that gets its peer info from the lighthouse?
//
// ANSWER: NO, AND THE BLAST RADIUS IS CHECKABLE. peerTrust has exactly ONE call site -- sysadminBridge.js:816,
// inside the callback that fires after a peer-update zip has finished downloading. server.html contains zero
// references to that path. The classification itself is pure string work on a URL: NO DNS, NO SOCKETS, NO
// NETWORK CALLS OF ANY KIND, so it cannot produce a network timeout even in principle. And the reported symptom
// predates the change, which the v3278 diagnosis already characterised: blocks that GROW every cycle, 487s
// blocked out of 500s of uptime. GROWTH IS THE TELL, and a once-per-pull cost is constant.
//
// *** BUT THE QUESTION FOUND A GENUINE DEFECT IN THE PEER-TRUST CODE, AND IT IS EXACTLY A WIRELESS-LAN DEFECT. ***
// A dual-stack Node server reports a LAN machine's req.socket.remoteAddress as ::ffff:192.168.1.50, and the
// lighthouse records precisely that as its remoteHint. peerOrigin handled the DOTTED v4-mapped spelling -- but
// new URL("http://[::ffff:192.168.1.50]").hostname NORMALISES TO ::ffff:c0a8:132, in hex, and the old code then
// read ".pop()" as "132" and matched nothing. So a wireless LAN peer reached by its mapped address classified
// EXTERNAL, required a signature, and had its updates STAGED INSTEAD OF APPLIED: a security rule doing its job
// on machines it was never meant to cover. Both spellings are now decoded, plus zone ids (fe80::1%wlan0), and
// the gate carries the shapes a real LAN produces rather than the ones that are easy to write. A PUBLIC address
// in mapped form (::ffff:8.8.8.8) still reads external -- the fix is not a skeleton key.
//
// ONE REAL COST, MEASURED AND KEPT: the trust check does a SYNCHRONOUS 24MB readFileSync of the downloaded zip
// on the event loop, plus a SHA-256 over it for external peers. Tens to low hundreds of milliseconds, ONCE PER
// PULL, not per poll. Worth moving off the main thread eventually; not the cause of anything continuous.
//
// STILL WORTH KNOWING: a LAN machine addressed by BARE HOSTNAME (http://swek-desktop:8080, no .local) classifies
// external BY DESIGN -- a hostname can resolve anywhere. On a network that names peers that way, those updates
// stage rather than apply. That is the fail-closed policy working as decided, not a bug, but it is a choice
// worth re-confirming now that the wireless case is understood.
// =================================================================================================================
// =================================================================================================================
// v3317 -- THE GROWING-BLOCK HYPOTHESIS, CHECKED. THE MEASUREMENT WAS RIGHT AND THE READING OF IT WAS WRONG.
//
// v3278 sampled /sys/lag on Keith's rig and recorded eight honest pairs of (uptime, cumulative blocked). It then
// summarised them as "487 SECONDS BLOCKED OUT OF ~500 SECONDS OF UPTIME", "the loop is dead ~97% of the time",
// and concluded the blocks GROW WITHOUT BOUND -- "it never settles, it gets worse forever". That conclusion has
// steered several rounds, including the boot-sidecar scope note, which explicitly declined to claim it addressed
// this stall.
//
// *** THE FINAL SAMPLE IS 138.5s BLOCKED OUT OF 498s. THAT IS 27.8%. *** The 97% figure is ROW ONE -- the first
// four seconds -- applied to the whole run. And the windowed duty cycle, the fraction of each interval spent
// blocked, FALLS from 65.6% to about 22% AND THEN HOLDS: 23.1%, 21.1%, 22.9% across the last three windows.
// Cumulative blocked grew 3.30x while uptime grew 5.79x; a runaway would need at least 33.5x.
//
// IF PER-CYCLE WORK GREW WITHOUT BOUND, THAT FRACTION WOULD RISE. It falls and plateaus. So the data shows TWO
// problems that were being treated as one:
//   (1) A HEAVY STARTUP BURST -- 97.5% blocked in the first four seconds. Real, serious, and exactly what the
//       boot sidecar addresses. The sidecar's scope note declined this stall on the grounds that "growth is the
//       tell"; on this reading it was entitled to claim the startup half all along.
//   (2) A STEADY ~22% PERIODIC DUTY CYCLE. Real, and still unmeasured on the rig. /sys/lag already names each
//       blocking callback by its REGISTRATION site, so the measurement that would settle it exists and has not
//       been taken.
//
// AND THE OBVIOUS SUSPECTS WERE RULED OUT BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN ARGUMENT. Two collections here grow with no
// pruning -- perf-ledger.json (checks[key].push, no cap) and the submissions archive (one file per submission,
// forever). A perf ledger of 90,000 samples parses in 76ms; a 20,000-file archive walks in 37ms; both linear.
// Each would need to be a THOUSAND times larger to explain a 138s block, so the search should not start there.
// A negative result, and worth as much as a positive one when it stops a hunt going the wrong way.
//
// HONEST LIMITS: eight samples, one rig, one run, cumulative counters rather than per-block durations.
// INDIVIDUAL blocks may well each be longer than the last -- v3278's actual observation -- while the duty cycle
// still falls, if the gaps grow too. What the numbers do not support is the conclusion drawn from them.
// The original samples are kept in place and the correction sits beside them: the measurement was fine, only the
// summary was wrong, and deleting the rows would have thrown away the evidence that showed it.
