// v3407 -- EULER BUCKLING: A THIRD EXACT KEY ON THE BEAM'S EXISTING OPERATOR, plus the CT gain law checked out of
//          sample.
//
// beam.js already answers two questions with one fourth-difference matrix: where a loaded beam ends up (static,
// FL^3/3EI) and how an unloaded one rings (the roots of cos b cosh b = -1). This adds a third -- WHEN DOES IT
// STOP BEING STABLE -- and the answer is exact: P_cr = pi^2 EI / (4L^2) = 2.467401, with higher loads at the ODD
// SQUARES so P2/P1 = 9 and P3/P1 = 25 EXACTLY.
//
// MEASURED: 8.0e-6 relative at n=160, converging at exactly 4.00x per grid doubling, with ratios 8.9994 and
// 24.9952. An integer is a far harder target than a fitted constant.
//
// WHY A THIRD KEY AND NOT A RESTATEMENT: v3319 showed the static and vibration keys catch DIFFERENT faults --
// one wrong boundary row broke both at once while giving each a plausible-looking wrong answer. A third
// independent question on the same matrix tests the operator. And it is independent in the same measurable way:
// a tenfold EI change scales every critical load by ten and moves the mode ratios only by round-off.
//
// *** THE FIRST GEOMETRIC STIFFNESS WAS HAND-ROLLED AND PRODUCED A GHOST. *** Written as second-difference rows
// with a ghost fold at the clamped end and a half cell at the free end -- mirroring K, which seemed like the
// careful thing to do -- it returned a lowest "critical load" of 0.152, then 0.076, then 0.038 as the grid
// refined. HALVING WITH n is the signature of a null-space artefact rather than a physical load, and it sat
// BELOW the true 2.467, where a reader finds it alarming rather than obviously wrong.
//   Written instead as the ENERGY FORM D^T D it is symmetric and positive definite BY CONSTRUCTION rather than
//   by hand-checking, and the free end needs no row at all because it is the NATURAL boundary of the energy.
//   That matters more than it sounds: under axial load the free-end shear condition gains a term in the slope,
//   since the axial force acquires a transverse component once the column tilts. A hand-written shear row
//   reusing the unloaded condition solves a subtly different problem and still returns a plausible number.
//
// AND THE CT GAIN LAW IS NOW CHECKED OUT OF SAMPLE. v3378 established that the FBP gain is a SAMPLING RATIO
// rather than a filter constant -- gain * nDet / N = 0.9456 +/- 0.0185 -- and answered NO to correcting it. That
// was a FIT, measured across N in {64,96,128} and nDet in {96,140,200} and checked on the same grid, which
// proves the arithmetic was done rather than that the law holds. At N=80/nDet=170, 112/210 and 72/260 -- none in
// the fitted set -- it predicts to within 0.45%. A fit that survives geometries it never saw is a law.
//
// (An independent measurement of the same constant during this session gave k = 0.945 +/- 0.016 against v3378's
// 0.9456 +/- 0.0185, by a different route. Two routes to the same number is the best available outcome, and the
// duplicate implementation was DISCARDED rather than landed -- only the out-of-sample evidence was kept.)
//
// A CLAIM OF MINE WAS TOO STRONG AND THE MEASUREMENT TRIMMED IT: I asserted the buckling mode ratios are
// BIT-IDENTICAL under a stiffness change, generalising from the vibration key where they happened to be. They
// are not. E cancels ANALYTICALLY -- the eigenvalues do not depend on it at all -- but each load is formed as
// mu * E * I / h^2 before the division, and (mu2 * c)/(mu1 * c) is not guaranteed to equal mu2/mu1 in IEEE. They
// agree to 4e-13, which is round-off, and that is what the check now says.
//
// Registry 64 -> 65 instruments. Knowledge index -> 748 gates.
