// v3561 -- THE SHADER ASKED FOR A TEST ON THE RIG. IT DID NOT NEED THE RIG, AND IT FAILS TWO WAYS.
//
// v3560's shader census found physics/octree/svo-raymarch.glsl: 88 lines of real WebGL2 that NOTHING LOADS,
// invisible to every census in the tree because no corpus included shaders. Its own header names the work it is
// owed -- "the RAY STEPPING is the part to validate on Galaxina against the CPU reader: for a set of rays, the
// voxel hit here must equal what a CPU march through svoAt reports."
//
// *** IT DOES NOT NEED A GPU, AND THAT IS THE THIRD TIME THIS SPLIT HAS PAID. *** tools/render-qa/checks.mjs is
// PURE over { data, width, height } and says so in its own header, so THE SAME notAllBlack THAT GRADES A
// SCREENSHOT ON GALAXINA GRADES A CPU RENDER HERE. v3501/v3502 fed it a converged path trace; v3559 used the
// same split for ddaFloat32. No pixel invariant is reimplemented -- the shipped checks are imported and run
// through runCheck, the manifest's own dispatcher.
//
// BOTH DEFECTS WERE PREDICTED FROM READING THE GLSL BEFORE ANY MIRROR EXISTED, THEN CONFIRMED.
//
// A -- *** THE MARCH CANNOT REACH THE FAR CORNER OF ITS OWN CUBE. *** 512 iterations at a step of 1.0/512.0
//   advance t by EXACTLY 1.0, and the [0,1] cube's diagonal is sqrt(3) = 1.7321. **42.26% of the longest
//   possible ray is never sampled**, and the loop exits on the ITERATION CAP rather than on `t > tExit`, so a
//   real voxel past that point renders as BACKGROUND -- A FALSE MISS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM EMPTY SPACE. Neither
//   number is mine: the reach is parsed out of the shader and the diagonal is the cube's own geometry.
//
//   DRIVEN, NOT DESCRIBED: geometry in the far corner with a diagonal camera gives 224 lit pixels from the
//   answer key and ZERO from the shader's arithmetic -- A BLANK IMAGE OF A WORLD THAT IS NOT BLANK -- with all
//   405 disagreeing rays of 2000 attributed to the budget and `other: 0`.
//
//   AND THE COMMENT BESIDE IT DESCRIBES SOMETHING THE CODE DOES NOT DO: "stepping by a fraction of the smallest
//   voxel". 1.0/512.0 is a fixed fraction of THE WHOLE CUBE and knows nothing of the tree's depth. At size 32
//   that is 16 samples per voxel; past size 512 the step is wider than a voxel and the march tunnels through
//   thin geometry. THE SAFETY IS AN ACCIDENT OF THE FIXTURE SIZE, NOT A PROPERTY OF THE CODE.
//
// B -- *** A FULLY SOLID WORLD RENDERS EMPTY. *** buildSVO has an explicit branch for a tree that is one uniform
//   leaf (a single node [0, color]) and svoAt has a matching special case. THE GLSL HAS NEITHER: it reads node
//   0, finds childMask 0, returns 0u. Measured -- svoAt says 1 at the centre of a solid world, the shader's
//   descent says 0, and all 9216 rays of the image report empty. A HANDLED BOUNDARY CASE ON ONE SIDE AND AN
//   UNHANDLED ONE ON THE OTHER: v3452's two meshers returning two shapes under one field name, in a new
//   subsystem.
//
// C -- *** THE WORST CASE IS NOT THE BLANK ONE. *** With geometry reaching closer, the shader draws 292 of 635
//   pixels -- MISSING 54% OF THE GEOMETRY -- and that image PASSES notAllBlack at nonBlackFrac 0.0317. A blank
//   image is at least suspicious; A PLAUSIBLE IMAGE SILENTLY MISSING MORE THAN HALF ITS GEOMETRY PASSES EVERY
//   WHOLE-IMAGE INVARIANT THERE IS. THE ANSWER KEY IS THE ROUND; a pixel invariant alone would never have found
