// v3574 -- THE TWO CONTROL QUANTITIES THAT ARE NOT INTEGERS, AND LYAPUNOV FINALLY REACHES THE SHIPPED RELAY.
//
// v3572 and v3573 built six roads to one integer and took integers first ON PURPOSE. What remained was everything
// control theory says that is CONTINUOUS, and the honest problem with a continuous quantity is that two routes to
// it agree to a TOLERANCE, and a tolerance is a number somebody chose.
//
// *** THE WAY OUT IS THAT THE GAIN MARGIN'S MEANING IS AN INTEGER EVEN WHERE ITS VALUE IS NOT. *** "Goes
// unstable" is the RHP count leaving zero, which v3572 answers exactly, so the second route NEVER EVALUATES A
// TRANSFER FUNCTION: it bisects K on Routh's verdict. A continuous number recovered from a yes/no -- navigate.js
// on the turning point, v3570 on the friction cone, again. AND IT LANDS ON THE EXACT RATIONALS: 60, 6, 30, 2, 8,
// the textbook values, from a search that never looks at a frequency.
//
// The frequency sweep reproduces them to 1e-9 -- ONCE THE PHASE CROSSING IS INTERPOLATED WITHIN THE GRID
// INTERVAL. Taking the nearest grid point instead leaves 5e-5 on a 400k-point log sweep, which is THE v3568
// PENDULUM LESSON A SECOND TIME: an uninterpolated crossing quantises to the sample spacing and the agreement
// collapses into the quantisation. The residual is shown to be the grid BY MOVING THE GRID rather than asserted.
//
// THEN THE MARGIN IS USED RATHER THAN MERELY COMPUTED TWICE: multiply the loop gain by 0.999 of it and every
// plant stands, by 1.001 and every plant falls, and AT the exact value every closed-loop polynomial is MARGINAL
// -- the row-of-zeros singular case v3572 built for, and THIS IS WHAT NEEDED IT. Every gain margin in control
// theory lives on that case.
//
// *** AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT CHECK WENT RED, CORRECTLY. *** It asked whether the BISECTED K was marginal.
// A bisection on a BINARY converges to a float arbitrarily near the transition, NOT ONTO IT, and the marginal set
// is MEASURE ZERO: four of five landed on the rational by luck of the arithmetic and the fifth missed by a ULP.
// The bisection's job is to BRACKET; asking a search to hit an exact algebraic point is asking for something it
// structurally cannot deliver. The check now uses the exact value and is written to go red if all five ever land.
//
// THE RELAY IS THE OTHER HALF, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT GRADES SHIPPED CODE. v3567 established that combat.js
// stepAI is a three-valued RELAY WITH A DEADBAND and NOT a Routh--Hurwitz subject; that reading has survived
// seven rounds and is not quietly reversed now. *** LYAPUNOV NEVER NEEDED LINEARITY, WHICH IS WHY IT IS THE ONE
// THAT ARRIVES. *** V = diff^2/2 gives V' = -turnRate*|diff|, which is bounded away from zero outside the band
// and does NOT vanish as diff approaches it -- so convergence is FINITE-TIME, not asymptotic, and that
// distinction is load-bearing: AN ASYMPTOTIC LAW WOULD HAVE THE SHIP APPROACHING ALIGNMENT FOREVER AND NEVER
// FIRING. The closed form is t* = (|diff_0| - deadband)/turnRate.
//
// *** IT IS GRADED BY IMPORTING ev/combat.js AND ev/flightModel.js AND RUNNING THEM. *** Both are pure and both
// are exported, so the prediction meets THE CODE THE GAME RUNS rather than a model of it -- every other control
// gate in this tree grades mathematics against mathematics. AND THE RESIDUAL IS AN EXACT TWO-SIDED BRACKET
// RATHER THAN A TOLERANCE: 0 <= t_simulated - t* < dt on all 32 case-and-timestep pairs, because a relay cannot
// stop mid-tick, so the simulated time is the analytic one ROUNDED UP TO THE NEXT TICK. That fails loudly if the
// relay ever overshoots the band, because the difference would then exceed dt. The ship is separately checked to
// END INSIDE the deadband rather than to have run out of ticks, and to land inside the FIRING cone -- which is
// v3572's 4 < 12 < 35 ordering stated as a consequence and measured instead of asserted about constants.
//
