// v3593 -- THE SECOND LAB-SCENE PROPOSER, REFUSING AT BOTH ENDS FOR TWO DIFFERENT REASONS.
//
// *** LOW mu FAILS BECAUSE A CONSTANT OFFSET OVER A SHRINKING KNOB EXPLODES. *** v3570 measured the residual as
// ADDITIVE at ~0.0102, and dividing that by mu gives 51% relative error at 0.02, 20% at 0.05, 10% at 0.1. The
// offset never shrinks; the knob does.
//
// *** AND HIGH mu FAILS BY GOING DEAF TO THE KNOB, WHICH IS v3588's ORBIT HAPPENING INSIDE ONE INSTRUMENT. ***
// tan(theta_c) reads 1.01359, 1.01266, 1.01266 at mu = 1.0, 1.3, 2.0 -- IDENTICAL TO FIVE DIGITS. The scene's
// block is a CUBE, and a cube topples at tan(theta) = 1 whatever the friction, so above that the bisection
// measures GEOMETRY rather than friction. Orbit was deaf across its WHOLE range and had to be refused entirely;
// pile goes deaf only above a boundary THE PHYSICS NAMES, so it is refused there and accepted below. And the
// saturation is detectable from the inside: THREE KNOB VALUES RETURNING ONE ANSWER IS A REPORT THAT THE KNOB HAS
// STOPPED BEING THE CAUSE -- a test any future candidate can be given.
//
// The topple check runs FIRST, on purpose. Running the bisection anyway returns 1.0127, which looks like a 49%
// error at mu = 2 and INVITES SOMEBODY TO WIDEN THE TOLERANCE UNTIL IT PASSED -- when the answer is not wrong,
// it is about something else.
//
// *** AND knobRegistry'S OWN GATE CAUGHT THE FIRST VERSION WITHIN MINUTES: "ALWAYS REFUSES: pile-friction". ***
// It returned pass:false when box3d was not loaded, and FROM THE OUTSIDE A DEPENDENCY FAILURE AND A PHYSICS
// VERDICT ARE THE SAME BOOLEAN. That gate exists to catch a control that cannot pass, and it caught one I had
// just written. The wasm loads at import now, so the dependency is a load-time fact rather than a per-call
// surprise. box3dNode's has() was fixed too -- it dereferenced a null module, so asking "can box3d do X" before
// loading it CRASHED instead of answering, and the whole point of a capability probe is to be safe to ask early.
//
// AND THE SCENE-TO-INSTRUMENT LINK WAS A GUESS. joinRegistered matched the SCENE ID against the proposer's
// instrument id and happened to work for gyroscope -- only because v3591 registered an instrument with that
// exact name. It broke the moment pile-friction was pointed at contact-keys, WHERE ITS KEY ACTUALLY LIVES, since
// v3570 built the friction cone there and not in a scene. proposers.mjs carries this lesson from v3286, when a
// stem match reported a false orphan: A LINK THAT HAS TO BE GUESSED IS NOT A LINK. The links are explicit now --
// and the check caught a bad one of mine WITHIN A MINUTE: I typed "blob-kelvin" for diffusion from the module
// name, and physics/blobKelvin.js has no instrument entry at all. It is null, because a scene owning no
// instrument should say so rather than match by luck.
//
// New: physics/pileKnob.mjs (adjudicate, score, propose, saturationProbe, ADDITIVE_OFFSET, TOPPLE_TAN) + its
// gate, 5 sections, 4.7s MEASURED; the pile-friction registration; explicit instrument links on 7 lab rows.
// Proposers 7 -> 8, lab scenes the panel can initiate 1 -> 2, instruments 141 -> 142.
// =================================================================================================================
const ENGINE_VERSION = "v3845";   // v3708 -- *** THE BLACKHOLE PLANT LANDED ON THE SECOND ATTEMPT, AND WHAT MADE IT POSSIBLE IS A MEASUREMENT THAT REFUTED MY OWN DIAGNOSIS TWICE. *** THE PLANT: rs = GM/c^2 INSTEAD OF 2GM/c^2, the most tempting slip in this corner of physics. EVERY SHAPE-OF-THE-ANSWER CHECK SURVIVES IT -- the potential is still -GM/(r - rs), orbits still bind, they still precess, energy conserves to 1e-10, capture still happens at SOME radius. Nothing looks wrong. IT REPLACES A CONSTANT IN A DEFINITION, NOT A LAW, which is why it is believable: a wrong law tends to look wrong and a wrong constant tends to look like an answer. rs was computed in FIVE PLACES and is now ONE function (this same file's v3421 note records what a third spelling already cost here once). *** THE KEY THAT CATCHES IT SEPARATES BY 118x: the onset mode BISECTS FOR THE CAPTURE BOUNDARY FROM THE SIMULATION and never reads the closed form, while iscoTheory is computed INDEPENDENTLY from G, M and c. Nominal: measured onset 6.0252 against theory 6, relErr 4.20e-3. Planted: the simulation stays perfectly SELF-CONSISTENT and lands on 3.0179 -- exactly 3 rs for the halved rs -- against a theory that still says 6, relErr 4.97e-1. THE PLANTED RUN IS NOT INCOHERENT, IT IS COHERENTLY WRONG, which is the only kind of plant worth having. plantedCoverage --verify: 31 -> 32 of 78. *** *** MY FIRST ATTEMPT AT THE BRACKET WAS WRONG AND ONE COMMAND SAID SO. The onset bisection starts at lo = 0.7 * iscoRadius(M,G,c); I called that "a search told roughly where to look", changed it to horizon*1.2, and THEN BOTH RUNS FAILED -- at r0 2.4 nothing captures at all. THE OLD BOUND WAS NOT ASSUMING THE ANSWER, IT WAS A BRACKET EMPIRICALLY KNOWN TO CONTAIN THE BOUNDARY. What it WAS doing wrong is narrower and realer: it reached for iscoRadius(M,G,c), WHICH RECOMPUTES 6GM/c^2 FROM THE CONSTANTS AND THEREFORE IGNORES THE rs THE RUN IS ACTUALLY USING. A SEARCH WHOSE BOUNDS COME FROM A CONSTANT THE RUN DOES NOT USE CAN ONLY FIND ANSWERS NEAR THE ONE ASSUMED. *** AND THEN THE SECOND ATTEMPT FAILED TOO, WHICH IS WHERE THE ACTUAL FINDING IS: CAPTURE IS A BAND, NOT A HALF-LINE. Swept in units of the ISCO -- at rs = 2 the particle captures at 0.7 and 0.9 and SURVIVES AGAIN AT 0.5; at rs = 1 it captures at 0.9 and survives at 0.7 and 0.5. THE BISECTION'S PREMISE IS MONOTONICITY AND IT IS FALSE NEAR THE HORIZON, so 0.7 sat inside the capturing band for the textbook rs BY LUCK. The bound is 0.9 of the ISCO IMPLIED BY THE rs IN PLAY, which captures for both; the nominal answer is unchanged to 0.4%. THE NON-MONOTONICITY IS RECORDED IN THE FILE RATHER THAN WORKED AROUND -- a bisection over a non-monotonic predicate is only trustworthy while its lower bound is inside the band, and NOTHING CHECKS THAT IT IS. *** WHAT SAVED THE ROUND WAS THE BIND REPORTING "onset-lo-survives" BY NAME instead of returning a number: an honest refusal beat a plausible figure twice in twenty minutes. AND ONE OF MINE, FILED AS A RULE: a python edit that asserts AFTER open(p,"w") LEAVES THE FILE EMPTY, and node --check PASSES ON AN EMPTY FILE -- blackHoleBind.mjs was silently zeroed and restored from the shipped zip. BUILD THE STRING FIRST, OPEN THE FILE LAST. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3707 -- *** I ASKED THE CURRICULUM WHAT TO DO NEXT, IT SAID "PLANT beam", AND READING beam STOPPED THE ROUND -- WHICH IS THE ROUND. *** beamBind's own line 106 already says it: "THE MEASURED REFUSAL: asymmetry is NOT selectively caught by reciprocity. Reported as data." The obvious plant -- break the free-end symmetry, the ACTUAL HISTORICAL BUG in that file, which converged cleanly to FL^3/(6EI) and "looks like a settled answer rather than a bug" -- HAD ALREADY BEEN TRIED AND MEASURED, and no key selectively separates it. Planting it anyway would have MANUFACTURED COVERAGE RATHER THAN EARNED IT. *** SO THE DEFECT IS IN THE PROPOSER, NOT THE BIND: it reads "is a plant DECLARED" and THAT CANNOT TELL AN UNEXAMINED SUBJECT FROM AN EXAMINED ONE THAT SAID NO. Same shape as every other collapse these notes record -- UNDECLARED is not DEAD, UNMEASURED is not EMPTY, UNKNOWN is not DESKTOP -- and this one sends a person to redo work that was deliberately not done, which is the most expensive kind of proposal because it looks like progress the whole way through. *** *** THE REFUSAL IS DECLARED AS A FIELD AND NEVER GREPPED OUT OF PROSE. beamDevice carries plantRefused, the curriculum reads it off the DEVICE (the registry hands out devices, so a module-level export would have been invisible to the very thing that needed it -- my first attempt did exactly that and found nothing), and refused subjects move to their own REFUSED bucket, PRINTED BY NAME with the reason. A PROSE GREP WOULD HAVE BEEN A MENTION TEST, the species of claim this session has got wrong three separate times. MEASURED: beam leaves the plant proposals and appears in the refused bucket; the next three become blackhole, blobbodies, blobkelvin. *** AND THREE ITEMS I CHECKED OFF THE OPEN LIST BEFORE PICKING WERE ALREADY CLOSED: notifyBridge's publish() HAS had a real caller since v3333 (the auto-update path, with its own gate line asserting it), the root brain.js duplicate IS GONE, and deadImportScan and moduleRefs are both GREEN. The list still says all three are open. CHECK EVERY NUMBER AGAINST THE TREE, NEVER THE RECORD -- the list says that about itself at the top, and it was right. NOT CLAIMED: that beam can never be planted -- the refusal is about the plant that was tried, and a DIFFERENT wrong derivation with a key that separates it would still be worth having. The declaration says what was measured, not that the subject is closed forever. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3706 -- *** ui/roundTrip.js IS FINALLY IMPORTED BY SOMETHING, AND ITS FIRST CONSUMER HAD THE EXACT BUG IT WAS WRITTEN FOR. *** Keith's six round-trip rules have sat implemented since v3243 with NO PAGE CALLING THEM -- the open list calls it "a real unwired helper, not a classification question". A round-trip control is one POPULATED FROM A FETCH AND READ BACK INTO A SAVE: if the fetch fails, the control holds a BROWSER DEFAULT and the next save writes that default as though somebody had chosen it. *** THE DIORAMA COMPOSER IS THAT SHAPE AND THE FAILURE IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL: boot() ends in `.catch(function(){ toUI({}); })`, so A FRESH BROWSER PLUS ONE FAILED FETCH fills every control with a default and THE NEXT KEYSTROKE AUTOSAVES A BLANK STAGE OVER diorama.json. I edited this file twice this session without seeing it. *** *** BOTH HALVES SHIP TOGETHER OR NEITHER WORKS. The rule is OMIT RATHER THAN SEND, and omitting is only protective IF THE RECEIVER KEEPS WHAT WAS NOT SENT -- otherwise a missing field is deleted exactly as thoroughly as a blanked one and the guard on the page is theatre. So the POST now MERGES when the body is flagged `partial`, and a FULL save still REPLACES: without that distinction, a normal save that legitimately removes every prop would have the removal merged away. AN EMPTY BODY IS ALSO NOT A LOADED COMPOSITION -- a box with no diorama.json answers {} happily, and calling that "loaded" walks straight back into saving defaults over nothing, which is harmless today and is THE SAME CODE PATH that overwrites a real one tomorrow. *** *** AND THE DRIVEN CHECK FORCED A CORRECTION THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX. My first version omitted fields only `if (RT)` -- guarded by the dynamic import of roundTrip.js -- and jsdom CANNOT RESOLVE A DYNAMIC IMPORT, so the page KEPT FLAGGING ITS SAVE AS PARTIAL WHILE SENDING A FULL BLANK BODY, and the server, told it was partial, MERGED THOSE BLANKS IN. THAT IS WORSE THAN NO GUARD, BECAUSE THE FLAG SAID IT WAS GUARDED. A SAFETY PROPERTY MUST NOT HANG OFF AN OPTIONAL IMPORT: the omit set is local now and works with or without roundTrip.js, and RT is used ONLY to decorate the controls so a person can SEE that a value is unknown rather than chosen. Second time in two rounds that jsdom's module limit exposed a real defect rather than a harness one. *** THE GUARD DOES NOT SWALLOW A REAL EDIT: each unknown flag clears on that control's first change, so the field the person actually touched IS sent while the four they never saw are not. gauges and props stay out entirely until something is loaded or added, because AN EMPTY props ARRAY FROM A FAILED LOAD IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM "I removed all the props" and sending it would do the latter. FIVE DRIVEN CHECKS with the load DELIBERATELY FAILING, and BOTH PLANTS FIRE: send the blank avatar anyway (the original bug) and one goes red; treat an empty server body as loaded and three do. NOT CLAIMED: that the other 102 round-trip controls across 34 files are fixed -- this is ONE FILE, which is what the list has said all along about the other thirty-three. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3705 -- *** THE ANSWER TO "IS GO/CONTROL FULLY WIRED IN" WAS A CLEAN NO, AND THE MEASUREMENT SAID SO IN ONE LINE: NOTHING CALLED /curriculum/run AND NO PAGE MENTIONED THE CURRICULUM AT ALL. *** The route has existed since v3699 and the fleet has been able to SEE a run since v3700, and the whole feature was still unreachable by a person -- a tool with a route, a run marker and its own gate, and no front door, which is the rule this project states most often. curriculum.html is that door. *** PHONE FIRST AND NOT AS A COURTESY: this is the page you open standing next to a machine, so ONE COLUMN is the layout and a wide screen just gets a wider column. NO TABLE -- a table of proposals is unreadable on a phone and nothing here needs columns. Touch targets are 44px, which is the smallest thing a thumb hits reliably; a 24px desktop button is the usual way a page that "works on mobile" turns out not to. The matching peer page is emphasised exactly as server.html does it (v3704), OFFERED and never navigated to, and an uncertain kind changes nothing. *** *** THE PROGRESS IS A PHASE COUNT AND THE PIPS ARE THE PHASES, read from THE SAME PLACE THE FLEET READS IT -- /fingerprint's activity block through activity.mjs's own describe(). A second reading of what "running" means, living in a page, is the duplicated-table class this tree has paid for repeatedly. NO SECONDS BAR: each census is one call that returns when it returns, and 96s is what THIS SANDBOX measured on one core. THE THREE OTHER STATES ARE RENDERED, NOT SWALLOWED -- NO GRADER, UNMEASURED and SKIPPED each appear by name, because one proposal with the skipped kind hidden would read as a frontier with almost nothing on it, and THE MOST EXPENSIVE LIE IS THE ONE THAT LOOKS LIKE COMPLETION. Every run ends by stating that a proposal means REACHABLE AND CHECKABLE, never worth doing. A 409 (a run already in flight) is shown rather than hidden. *** *** AND THE GATE CAUGHT THE THING I WOULD HAVE SHIPPED. Driven in jsdom, the Go button EXISTED AND PRESSING IT DID NOTHING -- because the page's script was type="module" AND jsdom DOES NOT RUN MODULE SCRIPTS, a limit written in my own notes. In a browser it would have worked; in the only place I can check, it was dead. A CONTROL NOBODY CAN PRESS IN A TEST IS A CONTROL NOBODY HAS PRESSED, and the check I had just written says a button wired to nothing renders identically to one that works. Converted to a CLASSIC script -- both module imports it needs are dynamic and already guarded, so nothing is lost, and a page whose entire script is a module also dies completely if that module fails to load. SIX DRIVEN CHECKS NOW PASS: Go asks the route, each proposal renders WITH its grader, the SKIPPED census is visible, the not-claiming line is present, and the doors checkbox REACHES the route as doors=1 rather than being decoration. *** ALSO CAUGHT: my first attempt to add those checks used an anchor that did not exist, so NOTHING WAS INSERTED and the suite passed unchanged -- which reads exactly like checks that fired. Confirmed by grepping for the new check's own name before believing the run. FIFTH TIME THIS SESSION. NOT CLAIMED: that any of it FITS A PHONE SCREEN. jsdom has no layout and no paint -- it can say a control exists and that pressing it asks, never that a thumb can reach it. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3704 -- *** THE PHONE IS OFFERED ITS OWN PEER PAGE, AND NOTHING IS TAKEN AWAY. *** Nothing in this tree had EVER branched on the device: the only matchMedia anywhere is stateOrb.js asking about prefers-reduced-motion. android-peer.html and ios-peer.html have BOTH been linked from the top of server.html all along, so a phone got the same two links as a workstation and had to know which one was for it. ui/deviceKind.mjs classifies and server.html EMPHASISES A LINK THAT WAS ALREADY THERE -- no redirect, nothing hidden, nothing rearranged. USER-AGENT SNIFFING IS WRONG REGULARLY (it is a string the browser volunteers, and desktop-mode toggles exist precisely so a phone can claim not to be one), SO THE COST OF A WRONG ANSWER MUST BE A HIGHLIGHT NOBODY WANTED, NEVER A PAGE NOBODY ASKED FOR. The tooltip carries the REASON, so on a device nobody here can see, "why is this glowing" is answerable without reading source. *** *** THE iPadOS 13 TRAP IS THE WHOLE REASON maxTouchPoints IS AN ARGUMENT: SINCE iPadOS 13 AN iPad'S USER AGENT SAYS "Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X" AND MENTIONS NO iPad ANYWHERE, so a string test alone calls THE MOST COMMON TABLET IN THE WORLD A DESKTOP. The discriminator is that an iPad reports maxTouchPoints 5 and a real Mac reports 0, and BOTH DIRECTIONS ARE CHECKED -- an iPad resolving correctly while a Mac ALSO resolved to ios would look identical on the case people test. With no touch count supplied the answer is "mac-or-ipad": A NAMED AMBIGUITY, NOT A GUESS, so THE ABSENCE OF A SIGNAL WIDENS THE ANSWER instead of narrowing it to whichever branch was written first. AN UNCERTAIN KIND IS OFFERED NO PAGE AT ALL -- pointing a probable Mac at the iOS page is wrong exactly when the person is least able to tell. *** UNKNOWN IS NOT DESKTOP. The tempting shape is `isPhone ? phone : desktop`, which turns EVERY FAILURE OF THE TEST into a confident claim about a workstation; an empty user agent, an unrecognised one and a missing navigator all return unknown. PLANTED: make unknown fall through to desktop and two checks go red. And ANDROID IS TESTED BEFORE LINUX because an Android user agent CONTAINS "Linux" -- the order of the tests IS the logic, and a Linux check placed first would swallow every phone in the fleet. The user agents in the gate are REAL STRINGS, not sketches: the case that matters most is the one nobody would invent, because it does not mention iPads. *** *** AND THE GATE CAUGHT A DEFECT IN ITSELF THAT IS WORTH MORE THAN THE ROUND. My server.html check sliced its window starting at the import path -- WHICH SITS INSIDE A STRING LITERAL -- so codeOnly's lexer BEGAN OUT OF PHASE AND INVERTED, keeping the strings and discarding the code: 972 characters down to 198. The "does not redirect" check then PASSED BY EXAMINING TEXT THAT NO LONGER CONTAINED ANY CODE, and a planted location.href DID NOT FIRE IT. A CHECK THAT PASSES BY LOOKING AT THE WRONG TEXT IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A CHECK THAT PASSED -- the same family as v3695's selector matching nothing and every() going vacuously true. The window now starts at the <script> tag so the lexer begins clean, and the redirect plant fires. sourceScan's own header warns that it desyncs; THIS IS WHAT THAT WARNING COSTS WHEN IGNORED. NOT DONE AND NOT PRETENDED: no phone-specific LAYOUT of the lab or the AI monitor -- this round is DETECTION plus an offer, and jsdom has no layout or paint, so whether anything FITS a phone screen remains Keith's to judge. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3703 -- *** ROUND THREE: A SMALL LOCAL MODEL PROPOSES A DIORAMA, THE VALIDATOR SAYS WHETHER IT IS BUILDABLE, AND NOBODY SAYS WHETHER IT IS GOOD. *** The order was the argument: v3701 built the checker, v3702 the presets, and only now the producer -- because A MODEL WHOSE OUTPUT IS ACCEPTED BECAUSE IT PARSED IS THE VOYAGER FAILURE THIS TREE CRITICISED (a skill kept because the code did not throw and a critic approved, which is the model judging its own work). Building the producer first is how the checker becomes optional. *** IT USES THE CALLER THAT ALREADY EXISTS: ollamaCaller.mjs (v2808), whose own header says the SweK gate decides right from wrong so it does not care who answered. NO SECOND CALLER. callModel is INJECTED exactly as escalateRoute injects its own, WHICH IS WHY THE GATE NEEDS NO MODEL -- every reply a small model really produces is a fixture: bare JSON, fenced JSON, JSON with an apology in front, an essay, a hallucinated mesh, colours in 0-255, a call that throws. A PRODUCER CHECKABLE ONLY AGAINST A LIVE OLLAMA WOULD NEVER BE CHECKED HERE AT ALL. *** *** THE REPLY IS NEVER REPAIRED, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING RULE. A code fence is stripped -- that is READING the reply -- but "Xbo" is NOT snapped to "Xbot" and a missing slot is NOT filled with a default. REPAIRING A REPLY IS THE PRODUCER BEING GRADED BY SOMETHING THAT ALREADY DECIDED TO AGREE WITH IT, and it hides the exact failure the validator exists to show. The composition comes back UNTOUCHED even when unbuildable, so a person can see what it tried and fix one slot; hiding it would leave "the model failed" with nothing to look at. PLANTED: snap an unknown avatar to the nearest real one and THREE checks go red. *** FOUR STATES BECAUSE THEY ARE FOUR DIFFERENT THINGS TO DO NEXT -- proposed / unbuildable / unreadable / NO-MODEL. "There was no model" is not "the model suggested nothing": a box with ollama switched off would otherwise look like a model with no ideas. SAME DISTINCTION the curriculum draws between UNMEASURED and an empty frontier, and the LAPACK grader between ABSENT and agreement. PLANTED: return an empty proposal instead and TWO checks go red. *** THE GATE CAUGHT TWO OF MINE. (1) `{"avatar":"Xbot"}` alone came back PROPOSED with zero problems, because the validator treats scene/gauges/props as OPTIONAL -- correct for a hand-edited layout, since the composer defaults what is missing, but a model that emits one field has done almost nothing and would have been ACCEPTED BECAUSE IT PARSED IN A DIFFERENT HAT. COMPLETENESS IS NOW REQUIRED OF THE PRODUCER, NOT OF THE FORMAT: the requirement lives in the proposer, so a person may still leave a slot to its default. (2) My route check searched codeOnly FOR A STRING LITERAL -- which codeOnly BLANKS -- so the index was -1 and the check failed for a reason unrelated to the route. codeOnly for an IDIOM, raw for a STRING; twenty-plus instances and I added another. *** NOTHING IS SAVED. The proposer has no write path (asserted through codeOnly so its own header cannot satisfy the check), the route writes nothing, and the composer FILLS THE CONTROLS AND WAITS -- the person still presses Apply, which is the whole difference between a proposal and a decision. The model name is TYPED, never guessed. NOT EXERCISED: no ollama in this sandbox, so the live half is UNRUN exactly as LAPACK is -- what is tested is the grader, and that is stated rather than implied. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3702 -- *** PRESETS, AND THE ROUND SHIPS NONE OF THEM. *** The composer started every session from whatever was in localStorage, so building a stage meant picking six slots cold; a named group you start from and then adjust is plainly better. THE PART WORTH BEING CAREFUL ABOUT IS WHERE THE GROUPS COME FROM. A PRESET IS TASTE, AND TASTE IS THE ONE THING I CANNOT DERIVE -- writing "Cosy Study" and "Lab Bench" out of my head is invented content nobody has read, the same class this tree forbids when it says never write a summary for a page that was never opened here. Six of mine would look like curation and be my guesses wearing Keith's project's clothes. SO THE MECHANISM SHIPS EMPTY, the empty list SAYS "no presets yet, save one" rather than showing a bare placeholder that reads as broken, and A GATE ASSERTS THE EMPTINESS -- checked through codeOnly so the header explaining why none ship cannot satisfy it, since that prose says "Cosy Study" out loud. PLANTED: ship one starter preset and FOUR checks go red. *** *** SAVE REFUSES WHAT AUTOSAVE ACCEPTS, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. v3701's POST /diorama/composition validates AND STILL WRITES, because it fires on every keystroke and refusing a half-finished edit would lose it. A PRESET IS NOT A KEYSTROKE -- it is somebody saying "this one is good, start from it later" -- so an unbuildable layout is REFUSED, the refusal NAMES THE SLOT so it can be fixed rather than leaving somebody wondering why nothing happened, and storing a broken one would only MOVE THE FAILURE TO WHOEVER APPLIES IT. *** AND A PRESET ROTS, WHICH IS WHY APPLY RE-VALIDATES AGAINST THE BOX AS IT IS NOW. One saved last month can name a mesh deleted since. It is STILL RETURNED, marked STALE with the problems attached, because the person can see it on screen and may want to load it and fix one slot -- refusing would lose work in front of them. WHAT MUST NOT HAPPEN IS APPLYING IT WITHOUT SAYING SO: the symptom otherwise is something QUIETLY MISSING FROM A PICTURE, the exact failure v3701 was built to stop. PLANTED: return stale:false and that check goes red. *** THE OPTION LISTS COME FROM THE DOM, not a second fetch: the avatar and prop selects are already filled from /avatar/imported and /assets/list, so the page reads the lists THE PERSON IS LOOKING AT. One source, and membership is checked against this box rather than against a copy that would drift. A preset name is deliberately narrow (letters, numbers, space, - and _) because it becomes both file content and an option value, and saving the same name REPLACES rather than duplicating -- two presets with one name is a select where the second is unreachable. *** NOT DONE: round three, the small local model that proposes a composition. It now has a gate to fail against, which was the whole reason for doing these in this order -- A MODEL WHOSE OUTPUT IS ACCEPTED BECAUSE IT PARSED IS THE VOYAGER FAILURE, and building the producer first is how the checker becomes optional. ALSO NOT DONE: the physics-lab go/continue control, and there is NO PHONE LAYOUT AND NO DEVICE DETECTION ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE -- see the notes. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3701 -- *** THE COMPOSITION VALIDATOR, BUILT FIRST ON PURPOSE AND WORTH HAVING EVEN IF THE OTHER TWO ROUNDS NEVER COME. *** diorama-composer.html has written { avatar, scene, pet, room, gauges[], props[] } to /diorama/composition since it existed AND NOTHING HAD EVER CHECKED IT: a layout naming an avatar that was since renamed, a gauge style that does not exist, or a prop mesh that was deleted saved happily and failed SILENTLY on the stage, where the only symptom is something missing from a picture. *** THE ORDER IS THE ARGUMENT. The plan is presets, then a small local model proposing compositions -- and A MODEL WHOSE OUTPUT IS ACCEPTED BECAUSE IT PARSED IS THE VOYAGER FAILURE THIS TREE CRITICISED THREE ROUNDS AGO (a skill kept because the code did not throw and a critic approved, which is the model judging its own work). JSON.parse succeeding is the weakest possible check, and BUILDING THE PRODUCER BEFORE THE CHECKER IS HOW IT BECOMES THE ONLY ONE. *** *** THE BOUNDARY IS THE FIRST CHECK IN THE GATE, NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT: it answers CAN THE STAGE BUILD THIS -- every name real, every number in range, nothing duplicated -- AND IT HAS NO OPINION ON WHETHER THE RESULT LOOKS GOOD. There is no answer key for a diorama; a diorama is taste and taste is Keith's eye. A VALIDATOR THAT PASSED SOMETHING UGLY IS WORKING CORRECTLY, and the passing report says so ON THE GREEN PATH where nobody is looking for caveats. *** THE OPTION LISTS ARE PASSED IN AND NEVER CARRIED HERE, asserted through codeOnly so no avatar name can hide in the file: the composer fills its selects from /avatar/imported and /assets/list AT RUNTIME, so the allowed sets are a property of THE BOX. A copy would drift the first time somebody imported a mesh and would then REJECT A PERFECTLY BUILDABLE LAYOUT -- worse than not checking at all. AND AN ABSENT LIST IS NOT AN EMPTY ONE: a box that has not answered /assets/list yet gets shape and range checked and MEMBERSHIP SKIPPED, because failing every layout on a slow boot teaches somebody to ignore the check. UNKNOWN IS NOT ABSENT, again. *** TEN PROBLEM CLASSES ARE DRIVEN plus the one that matters most: THE GAUGE ARRAY IS POSITIONAL -- index i IS vital i, there is no name on an entry -- so dropping one does not throw, it SILENTLY RE-ASSIGNS EVERY GAUGE AFTER THE GAP. A missing gauge is not a missing gauge, it is FIVE WRONG ONES, and nothing in the stage would report it. IT RETURNS EVERY PROBLEM, NOT THE FIRST: a producer needs the whole list to fix in one pass, and a trickle of one-at-a-time failures looks like progress. *** THE SAVE ROUTE VALIDATES AND STILL SAVES, DELIBERATELY. That POST is the composer's own autosave on every keystroke, and REFUSING A HALF-EDITED LAYOUT WOULD LOSE THE EDIT -- a person editing their own diorama gets TOLD, not blocked. The producer that must not proceed on a red result is the model coming later. A validator failure cannot cost anybody their layout either: it is caught and the write proceeds. NOT DONE: presets and the model are rounds two and three, and the physics-lab go/continue control is a fourth -- physics-lab.html and instruments.html ALREADY EXIST, so the central front end was never missing. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3700 -- *** I SAID NO PAGE RENDERS THE RUN. THE PAGE HAS RENDERED IT SINCE v3020 AND I HAD NOT LOOKED. *** server.html's Roundhouse gauge reads THIS box's activity through the same module the peer publishes with, and prints "this box: running -- <device> round N/M" plus a transcript link. So the answer to "should the peer row show a curriculum phase" was: IT ALREADY WOULD, AND IT WOULD SAY TWO WRONG THINGS. CHECK THE TREE, NOT THE RECORD -- and the record here was my own sentence from one round ago. *** AND THE BIGGER FINDING IS WHY NOBODY EVER NOTICED: NOTHING IN THE TREE HAS EVER SET __swekActiveRun. fingerprintBridge has READ it since v3018 and the panel since v3020, and v3699's curriculum route is THE FIRST WRITER IN SIX HUNDRED VERSIONS. A READER WITH NO WRITER -- so the whole "running" branch, its round counter and its transcript link are code NOBODY HAS EVER SEEN RUN, and every box in the fleet has reported idle since v3018 because idle was the only thing it could report. THE FIRST RUN TO REACH THAT BRANCH IS THE ONE WHOSE LABEL AND LINK ARE BOTH WRONG FOR IT, which is how both defects surfaced in the same minute. *** *** TWO THINGS WEARING ONE LABEL, ARRIVING THE MOMENT A SECOND KIND OF RUN EXISTED: a roundhouse ROUND is an iteration of propose-verify-escalate; a curriculum PHASE is one census returning. The unit is now CARRIED ON THE RUN BLOCK, NOT MATCHED ON THE DEVICE NAME -- a name test is exactly what this session got wrong three times (the mac scan, the "27 of 48" grep, the load-bearing-negative phrase). *** AND THE LINK IS THE WORSE HALF: the panel offered /run-inspector.html?id=... for ANY running box, and A CURRICULUM RUN WRITES NO TRANSCRIPT. A LINK TO EVIDENCE THAT DOES NOT EXIST IS WORSE THAN NO LINK -- it looks like corroboration. `transcript` is now OPT-IN and DELIBERATELY NOT DEFAULTED TRUE, so a future run type that forgets to declare one is simply not offered a link rather than being handed a dead one. PLANTED: flip the default to `!== false` and TWO checks go red. *** ONE PLACE TURNS A BLOCK INTO WORDS. server.html built that string inline, which is precisely why it could never be checked -- activity.describe(a) returns { text, link } and the panel renders what it is given. The five new checks live in activity-selfcheck beside the module, and they are the first checks this feature has ever had. *** NOT CLAIMED: that it LOOKS right. jsdom has no layout and no paint, and the panel is Keith's to judge -- what is proven is that the words and the link are correct for both kinds of run and that an idle box still says idle while a peer that reported NOTHING still says unknown (an older build, not an idle box, which describe must not flatten). CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3699 -- *** A CURRICULUM RUN IS NOW VISIBLE TO THE WHOLE FLEET, AND IT NEEDED NO NEW CHANNEL. *** Keith asked whether a connected peer starting a proposer run would show on the peer network and server.html. CHECKING THE TREE ANSWERED IT: activity.mjs has asked "WHAT IS THIS PEER DOING RIGHT NOW?" since v3018, fingerprintBridge already publishes activityOf(globalThis.__swekActiveRun), and every peer already reads that block. WHAT WAS MISSING WAS ONLY A RUN THAT SETS THE MARKER. Building a second activity feed -- which is exactly what I would have done without looking -- would have been TWO DECLARATIONS OF ONE THING, this tree's most repeated defect, in the round that adds the thing being declared. *** THE ROUTE OWNS THE MARKER AND THE CURRICULUM STILL CANNOT PUBLISH ITSELF. v3698's whole safety property is that the proposer HAS NO WRITE PATH -- a loop that generates work must not be able to mark it done -- and a module that publishes its own progress is a module with state somebody can reach. So GET /curriculum/run sets and clears globalThis.__swekActiveRun; the curriculum takes an onPhase CALLBACK and never learns what the caller does with it. IT CALLS BACK; THE CALLER DECIDES WHAT THAT MEANS. A plant proves the split: give curriculum.mjs a markRun() that touches the global and the gate goes red. *** *** AND THE ANSWER TO THE TIMER QUESTION IS NO, DELIBERATELY, WHICH IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF. A 90-SECOND VIRTUAL BAR WOULD BE A DRAWING RATHER THAN A MEASUREMENT: 96 seconds is what THIS SANDBOX measured on ONE CORE, and a countdown hard-coded from it would be wrong on every other box -- A NUMBER THAT LOOKS LIKE A MEASUREMENT AND IS A MEMORY OF SOMEBODY ELSE'S MACHINE, which is the shape these notes name most often. WHAT IS HONESTLY AVAILABLE IS A PHASE COUNT, and it is real: each census is ONE OPAQUE CALL that returns when it returns, so "2 of 3" is the finest true thing there is. activity.mjs HAS CARRIED round/maxRounds SINCE v3018 FOR EXACTLY THIS, and marks a run UNATTENDED when nothing has ticked it for fifteen minutes. The total counts only kinds that will actually be ATTEMPTED, so a SKIPPED slow census never inflates the denominator. *** THE MARKER IS CLEARED IN A finally, and that line is there because A PLANT DID NOT FIRE. The driven check sets and clears the marker ITSELF, so it proves the pattern and would sail straight past a route that forgot its clear -- which is precisely the failure that leaves a whole fleet reading RUNNING on a box that finished fifteen minutes ago. The route's OWN SOURCE is now asserted as well, and deleting its finally goes red. FIFTH TIME THIS SESSION THAT A PLANT NOT FIRING WAS THE FINDING. *** NOT DONE AND NOT PRETENDED: no page renders the new run yet -- the fleet DATA carries it because the fingerprint does, and whether server.html's peer row should show a curriculum phase beside a roundhouse round is a UI decision I cannot see the result of. AND ONLY ONE RUN AT A TIME PER BOX: a second request gets 409 rather than two runs sharing one marker and reporting each other's progress. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3698 -- *** THE CURRICULUM: WHAT TO ATTEMPT NEXT, DERIVED FROM WHAT IS ALREADY MEASURED -- AND IT NEVER GRADES ANYTHING. *** Voyager (MineDojo, MIT) has three parts and SweK already had two: skillbook.mjs is the skill library and the roundhouse ends in a real gate. THE PART NEVER BUILT WAS THE FIRST ONE -- nothing proposed what to attempt next from the capability frontier. Voyager is REFUSED as code (Python + Node + A LICENSED COPY OF MINECRAFT with Fabric mods and a GPT-4 key); only the idea travelled, which is the heidler/elliptic rule again. *** AND ON THE PART BOTH HAVE, SweK'S IS THE STRONGER DESIGN FOR A SPECIFIC REASON: Voyager keeps a skill when the generated code did not throw and an LLM critic approves -- THE MODEL JUDGING ITS OWN WORK. skillbook certifies only when a MEASURED before/after clears a size bar AND a noise bar (Z_MIN 2.33), and carries UNDERPOWERED for "measured, too small to decide", which Voyager has no state for. *** *** THE DANGER SHAPED EVERY LINE: A LOOP THAT GENERATES ITS OWN WORK CAN ALSO MARK IT PASSED. So the file HAS NO WRITE PATH AT ALL -- no baseline, no coverage register, no SWEK_FREEZE lever -- and its gate asserts that BY MECHANISM through codeOnly, so the prose describing what it must not do cannot satisfy the check. A TASK WITH NO NAMED GRADER IS NOT PROPOSED: every proposal carries the gate that would decide it and that gate must EXIST ON DISK. NOTHING IS INVENTED: the gate asserts no proposed subject appears as a string literal in the proposer, and that every GRADE proposal is a member of gradedCoverage's own ungraded set. PLANTS FIRE BOTH WAYS -- give it a writeFileSync and the safety check goes red; make one census prepend a subject no register contains and the derivation check goes red. *** THE THREE STATES ARE SEPARATE ON PURPOSE AND THAT IS THE SUBTLEST PART: PROPOSED, UNGRADABLE (the checker does not exist) and UNMEASURED (the census could not be READ). A curriculum answering "nothing to do" when a census merely failed to load would be THE MOST EXPENSIVE POSSIBLE LIE, because it looks like completion. UNKNOWN IS NOT ABSENT, in the file most tempted to collapse it. And every run ends by saying what it is NOT claiming: a proposal says a subject is REACHABLE and CHECKABLE, never that it is WORTH DOING -- that judgement is Keith's, and a ranked list gets read as a recommendation whatever the header says. *** *** AND THE FIRST BUILD WAS TOO SLOW TO BE A TOOL, WHICH IS A FINDING RATHER THAN AN INCONVENIENCE. orphanTriage.actionable() WALKS THE WHOLE TREE AND TAKES OVER 285 SECONDS, so the door kind alone timed the gate out at 290s; one propose() cost ~96s and the gate calls it five times. All three censuses are now MEMOISED PER PROCESS (never to disk -- a cache across runs would advise from a frontier that had already moved), and the DOOR KIND IS OPT-IN behind --doors, SKIPPED BY NAME rather than silently dropped. Default run 96s, gate 88s. A CURRICULUM NOBODY RUNS PROPOSES NOTHING -- the same treatment labResults gets for its nine minutes -- BUT A KIND THAT VANISHED QUIETLY WOULD READ AS A FRONTIER WITH NOTHING ON IT. NOT CLAIMED: that the proposals are good ones. It ranks by reachable-and-checkable, and it has no opinion beyond that. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3697 -- *** KEITH ASKED WHETHER "NOBODY HAS CLICKED THEM ON A REAL BOX" WAS A ROUND FOR HERE OR SOMETHING FOR HIM, AND THE HONEST ANSWER SPLIT IN TWO: THE WIRING IS MINE AND ONLY THE LOOK IS HIS. *** I had written that the other four pickers were "wired the same way" as the grab one -- ON MY WORD ALONE, since only grabDialog was ever driven. THE WIRING IS EXACTLY THE PART THAT CAN BE SILENTLY WRONG, and it is the part I claimed to have fixed: an off-by-one sending a file, or an unattended remote-view request, to the wrong machine. *** AND THEY COULD NOT BE CHECKED AS WRITTEN, WHICH IS ITSELF THE FINDING: both flows were inline inside their click handlers, READING `peerList` OUT OF A CLOSURE THAT ONLY A FETCH FILLS. No fixture can reach a closure variable. A FUNCTION THAT TAKES ITS INPUT IS TESTABLE; ONE THAT READS A CLOSURE IS NOT -- so sendPickers(entries, peers) and rdPickPeer(peers) take their lists as PARAMETERS now, and the handlers pass what they already had. *** *** TWO REAL LOG LINES CAME OUT OF NEEDING SOMETHING TO WATCH, and neither is test-only state: the send progress line now reads "sending notes.txt -> http://..." and rdRequestView LOGS the destination before the request goes out. THE PANEL PREVIOUSLY NEVER SAID WHICH PEER GOT THE FILE OR WHICH ONE WAS ASKED TO CONNECT -- so the thing that makes the check possible is the same thing a person wanted anyway, which is the only kind of test hook worth adding. *** FIVE NEW DRIVEN CHECKS, AND THE FIXTURE PICKS ROW TWO OF EACH LIST ON PURPOSE: two files (folder first, then notes.txt) and two peers, choosing the SECOND of each. WITH ROW ONE A HARD-CODED FIRST-ENTRY BUG WOULD PASS -- which is precisely how the index prompt failed in the first place. The checks assert that step 1 lists files folders-first, that step 2's rows each carry their OWN url, that the SECOND file reaches the SECOND peer, and that the RustDesk request carries the clicked peer's url. *** BOTH PLANTS FIRE: make the send always use peers[0] and the file/peer check goes red; make RustDesk always ask peers[0] and the request check goes red. THE FIRST PLANT'S ANCHOR MISSED ON THE FIRST ATTEMPT AND THE RUN CAME BACK GREEN -- WHICH IS EXACTLY WHAT A PLANT THAT DOES NOT FIRE LOOKS LIKE. It was re-applied BY LINE INDEX and CONFIRMED BY GREP before being believed, which is v3683's lesson and the fourth time this session it has earned its place. *** WHAT IS STILL KEITH'S AND CANNOT BE ANYTHING ELSE: whether the overlays LOOK right, whether a real transfer completes, and whether the RustDesk request lands on the machine he clicked. jsdom has NO LAYOUT AND NO PAINT -- it can say a row exists and that clicking it carries the right value, never that the dialog fits the screen or that the file arrived. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3696 -- *** THE FIFTH AND LAST TYPE-A-NUMBER PROMPT IS A PICKER, AND IT WAS THE WORST OF THE FIVE. *** RustDesk "Request View" read "Type a number or paste a peer URL", so choosing a peer meant COUNTING DOWN A LIST IN A TEXT BOX and typing an index. AN OFF-BY-ONE HERE ASKS THE WRONG MACHINE TO OPEN A REMOTE-VIEW SESSION POINTED AT THIS ONE -- worse than the misdirected file send v3694 fixed, because it is an unattended screen-sharing request to a box nobody chose, and the only sign is a request landing somewhere unexpected. Clicking a peer row carries its OWN url; there is no index arithmetic left. The request itself is UNCHANGED: same /rustdesk/self read, same POST to the peer's /rustdesk/connect, same button states. ONLY HOW THE DESTINATION IS CHOSEN MOVED -- and it goes through the SAME pickFromList the four file and peer pickers use, so this panel now has ONE picker, ONE comparator and ONE overlay rather than five hand-rolled prompts. *** *** THE RATCHET IS ON THE TELL, NOT ON THE COUNT, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. server.html still has FOUR prompt() calls AND ALL FOUR ARE HONEST: the engine password, the idle-lock minutes, A PATH ON ANOTHER MACHINE THIS BOX CANNOT LIST, and naming a peer -- free text with nothing to pick from. A count-based ratchet would have had to bless four and would go red the day somebody added a legitimate fifth. What must never come back is A PROMPT STANDING IN FOR A PICKER, and all five that did said the same thing -- "type a number" or "type the exact name" -- BECAUSE THE PAGE ALREADY HAD THE LIST AND ASKED THE READER TO TRANSCRIBE FROM IT. That phrase is the defect's signature and it is what the check hunts. The gate names the correct response in advance: if it goes red, THE FIX IS A pickFromList CALL, NOT A REWORDED PROMPT. *** IT READS noComments AND NOT codeOnly, DELIBERATELY AND FOR THE DOCUMENTED REASON: the text being hunted IS A STRING LITERAL, so codeOnly would blank the very thing being looked for. And the prose ABOVE the grab picker DESCRIBES the old prompt in a comment -- on the first attempt that comment counted as a prompt, which is the prose-as-code trap in the check written to close a prompt. BOTH DIRECTIONS SHOWN: plant a "Type a number" prompt back in and it fires; the surviving prose comment does NOT count, checked as a control beside it. *** THE SEAM BROKE THE SAME WAY IT DID AT v3694 AND WAS CAUGHT THE SAME WAY: converting a click handler into a named function leaves the handler's own `});` closing a plain function, and all 39 inline scripts were parsed to find it. THAT PARSE IS THE ONLY THING BETWEEN A REFACTOR LIKE THIS AND A DEAD PANEL, since nothing else in the ship ritual reads server.html as JavaScript. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3695 -- *** grabDialog IS NOW A CALLER, NOT A SECOND PICKER -- THE THING v3694 SAID IT HAD NOT DONE. *** v3694 extracted pickFromList FROM THIS FUNCTION'S SHAPE and then left the original standing, so server.html carried TWO pickers: the exact defect the extraction existed to prevent. That entry said so plainly rather than claiming the tidy version, and this round is the debt being paid rather than re-described. grabDialog is now eleven lines that call pickFromList with the same sort, the same labels, the same empty notice and the same POST. *** WHAT IS UNCHANGED AND HAD TO STAY SO: folders first via dirFirst -- THE ONE COMPARATOR BOTH FILE LISTS USE, so they cannot disagree about ordering the first time somebody edits one -- the folder/file glyphs from fileLabel, the empty notice, and /peer/grab with {peer, path} where path is the entry's OWN name. The behaviour is identical; only where the code lives moved. *** *** THE FOUR JSDOM CHECKS WERE REWRITTEN IN THE SAME EDIT, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART. They asserted on data-grab-name, data-grab-dir, #sgdProg and #sgdEmpty, and the folded picker emits data-pick-name, data-pick-dir and <id>Prog / <id>Empty. LEAVING THEM POINTING AT THE OLD ATTRIBUTES WOULD HAVE GONE GREEN BY MATCHING NOTHING -- querySelectorAll returns an empty list, every() over it is vacuously true, and a refactor nobody is watching any more would have looked exactly like a refactor that worked. THIS FILE HAS RECORDED THAT FAILURE MODE AGAINST ITSELF TWICE ALREADY. The assertions are the SAME CLAIMS -- rows are buttons, folders first, the click acts on THAT entry, an empty list says so -- and only the spelling of where to look changed. *** pickFromList NOW COPIES THE dir FLAG THROUGH when an entry has one, because a FILE entry carries dir and a PEER entry does not, and a caller (and the gate driving it) must still be able to tell a folder from a file WITHOUT a second dialog type existing. *** THREE PLANTS, ALL FIRING, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each: stop copying the dir flag and the folders check goes red; drop the sort from grabDialog's call and it goes red; make the click send entries[0].name instead of the clicked entry and the click check goes red. THE FIRST TWO MATTER MOST HERE -- they are the two ways a fold like this quietly loses a property while every row still renders, and a screenshot would show nothing wrong in either case. *** STILL NOT CONVERTED, STILL COUNTED: the RustDesk "Request View" prompt, which asks you to type a number or paste a peer URL. FOUR OF FIVE typed prompts in that panel are pickers now. The Host-path prompt in sendDialog STAYS ONE ON PURPOSE -- it names a path on ANOTHER machine this box cannot list, so there is nothing to pick from and a text box is the honest control. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3694 -- *** THE TWIN I NAMED AT v3692 WAS NOT A TWIN, IT WAS THREE MORE, AND ONE OF THEM COULD SEND A FILE TO THE WRONG MACHINE. *** v3692 replaced the peer GRAB prompt and recorded that paSelfSend still typed. Reading it properly found THREE typed prompts, not one: open a local file, choose what to send, and CHOOSE THE DESTINATION PEER -- that last one reading "type a number or paste a peer URL", so picking a peer meant COUNTING DOWN A LIST IN A TEXT BOX and typing an index. AN OFF-BY-ONE THERE SENDS YOUR FILE TO THE WRONG MACHINE, SILENTLY, WITH THE SEND REPORTING SUCCESS -- there is no confirmation step and no name in the result to notice it by. Clicking a peer row carries its OWN url and there is no index arithmetic left to get wrong. All three now go through a picker, in two labelled steps for the send. *** THE POSTS ARE UNCHANGED -- /peer/dl/get, /peer/send, same routes, same body shapes. ONLY HOW THE TWO VALUES ARE CHOSEN MOVED, which is the whole claim: this round changed no behaviour a server can see. ONE COMPARATOR ORDERS BOTH FILE LISTS (folders first) rather than two, because two would disagree the first time somebody edited one. *** *** AND I DID NOT FINISH THE JOB I SET OUT TO DO, WHICH IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN IMPLIED BY A TIDY NOTE. pickFromList was EXTRACTED FROM grabDialog'S SHAPE AND grabDialog HAS NOT BEEN FOLDED INTO IT -- so there are now TWO pickers in this file, which is the exact defect the extraction existed to prevent, and my first draft of this entry claimed "one picker, four call sites". IT IS NOT TRUE YET. Folding grabDialog in also means rewriting the four jsdom checks that assert on its data-grab-name attributes, and half-doing that would break a picker that currently works and is driven. NAMED, OWED, NEXT. *** ALSO NOT CONVERTED AND NOW COUNTED: THE RUSTDESK "REQUEST VIEW" PROMPT HAS THE SAME type-a-number DEFECT (line 4560). That makes the running total FIVE typed prompts in this one panel, of which FOUR are now pickers and one is not. The Host-path prompt in sendDialog STAYS A PROMPT ON PURPOSE -- it names a path on ANOTHER machine that this box cannot list, so there is nothing to pick from and a text box is the honest control. *** THE ANCHOR MISSED TWICE AND THE ASSERTION CAUGHT IT BOTH TIMES: the file stores an EM DASH as a literal character where I had written \u2014, so the first replacement matched nothing and the script exited before writing -- confirmed by grepping the count rather than by the absence of an error, which is v3683's lesson applied on purpose this time. The second attempt left a MALFORMED SEAM (the old promise chain's tail hanging under a new function), caught by parsing all 39 inline scripts, which is the check that would have shipped a dead panel otherwise. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3693 -- *** THE SSIM PAGE: TWO IMAGES, THREE NUMBERS, AND THE MAP THAT IS THE ONLY ONE OF THEM THAT SAYS WHERE. *** ssim-compare.html runs render/perceptual.mjs's GLOBAL ssim() and render/ssimWindowed.mjs's windowed form ON THE SAME PAIR and draws the local map as a heatmap with the worst window boxed. Built-in demonstration pair (a 256x256 render with an 8/16/32/64 corner inverted) so it works with zero setup and shows the exact case that motivated v3691, PLUS two file inputs so Keith can drop in real renders -- which is the "real life view comparison" that was asked for. *** THE PAGE PICKS NO THRESHOLD AND SAYS SO. A single bad window can be a real defect or one legitimately noisy corner; the page shows the distribution and where the worst one is, and the cut stays Keith's. THE HEATMAP RAMP IS FIXED AT [-1, 1] RATHER THAN NORMALISED to whatever this pair produced -- AUTO-SCALING WOULD MAKE A CLEAN COMPARISON LOOK AS ALARMING AS A BROKEN ONE, which is the opposite of what anybody reads a heatmap for. The size check is the MODULE'S and its message is shown as-is, so there is ONE rule about what is comparable rather than a second one in the page. *** *** AND THE MODULE LEAVING THE ORPHAN PILE IS WHAT CAUGHT MY OWN STALE ENTRY. graveyard went 87 -> 86 the moment a page imported ssimWindowed, and then went RED: "no register entry has outlived its reason -- STALE, DELETE THESE: render/ssimWindowed.mjs". I WROTE THAT ENTRY LAST ROUND AND THE GATE MADE ME DELETE IT ONE ROUND LATER, which is exactly what unwiredRegister's header tells the next person to do -- AN ENTRY THAT SUPPRESSES NOTHING WOULD SILENTLY SWALLOW THE NEXT REAL ONE. Deleted, not edited, with the reasoning kept in its place. *** THE BLOCKER DID NOT EVAPORATE, IT MOVED AND IT IS NARROWER NOW: the entry said the hold-up was A VERDICT CHANGE, and A PAGE CHANGES NO VERDICT. What is still open is whether backendVisualDiff or render-qa should GRADE on the worst window, which WOULD re-fail renders that pass today. That decision is on the open list, beside the page that makes it visible. *** ALSO VERIFIED THIS ROUND, NOT ADOPTED: WICG/html-in-canvas -- drawElement / drawElementImage / layoutsubtree, real, a living explainer, and IMPLEMENTED IN CHROMIUM ONLY BEHIND A FLAG (chrome://flags/#canvas-draw-element, Chrome Canary and Brave 147+). Gecko NO SIGNAL, WebKit NO SIGNAL. It is genuinely the DOM-to-canvas capture backend-dom.html and render QA would want, and IT MUST NOT BECOME AN INPUT TO A GATE: a comparison fed by a flag-gated API in one engine can only be produced on one browser with a flag flipped, which is a MIRROR OF A NEW KIND -- a measurement nobody else can reproduce. A DEMO IS THE ONLY HONEST USE UNTIL A SECOND ENGINE SHIPS IT. -> [[external-repos-4]] CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3692 -- *** THE PEER GRAB PICKER, WHICH DID NOT EXIST. *** Keith: choosing a peer and asking to GET a file gave no file selection at all. HE WAS RIGHT AND IT IS NOT A BROKEN PICKER, IT IS THE ABSENCE OF ONE: peerFiles() fetched /peer/dl/remote, JOINED THE ENTRY NAMES INTO A STRING, and put them inside a prompt() -- so you READ a list out of a one-line text box and TYPED THE EXACT NAME BACK, spaces and all. THE ENDPOINT HAS ALWAYS RETURNED {name, dir} ENTRIES, so everything a picker needs was already on the wire and had been for as long as the feature existed. Now a clickable row per entry, folders sorted FIRST and marked, because /peer/grab takes either AND A FOLDER GRAB IS THE EXPENSIVE ONE -- a list that hides which is which invites the accidental one. An EMPTY listing SAYS SO rather than showing an empty box: an empty Downloads folder is not an error and is not a picker either, and saying which is the difference between a reader waiting and a reader knowing. *** NO LIBRARY, AND elFinder WAS THE ALTERNATIVE CONSIDERED. VERIFIED AND REFUSED: BSD-3-Clause and real, but its CLIENT needs jQuery >= 1.8 AND jQuery UI >= 1.9 (this tree is vanilla ES modules with no framework) and its OFFICIAL CONNECTOR IS PHP 5.2+ (this server is Node -- third-party connectors exist for Java, .NET and Python, NONE OFFICIAL FOR NODE). It is a file MANAGER (thumbnails, upload policy, trash, netmount) and the gap was A PICKER. Two frontend dependencies and a connector protocol to replace forty lines. *** *** IT IS DRIVEN IN A REAL DOM, NOT DESCRIBED. A picker is a DOM claim and placementRender-selfcheck is the only file here with a DOM, so grabDialog is exposed on window and the gate BUILDS IT AND CLICKS IT: four entries become four BUTTONs, the two folders sort first and carry the folder glyph, clicking the THIRD row puts "grabbing beta.bin" in the progress line SYNCHRONOUSLY (before any fetch resolves, so the wiring is proven without a network), and an empty list renders the empty notice. Plus a source check that the typed-name prompt is gone, because THE DOM CHECK CANNOT SEE A PROMPT THAT IS NEVER REACHED. BOTH PLANTS FIRE: drop the folders-first sort and 2 checks go red; make the click send rows[0].name instead of the clicked entry's and 1 goes red -- the second is the one that matters, since a picker that always grabs the first thing would look perfect in a screenshot. *** ONE OVERLAY SHELL NOW SERVES BOTH DIRECTIONS. sendDialog had built its own and grab had none; writing a second would have been two spellings of one dialog. ONLY THE SHELL MOVED -- every behaviour line in sendDialog is untouched, and the send path was READ AND FOUND CORRECT rather than assumed broken: it builds real hidden <input type=file multiple> and a second with webkitdirectory and calls .click() inside a user gesture. IF SEND IS ALSO FAILING ON KEITH'S BOX THAT IS A BROWSER-SIDE QUESTION AND NEEDS HIS CONSOLE; I will not call code broken that reads correct. *** AND A THIRD PATH HAS THE SAME DEFECT AND IS DELIBERATELY LEFT: paSelfSend (send FROM local Downloads) uses TWO typed prompts -- one for the file and one asking you to "type a number or paste a peer URL" for the destination. IT IS THE WORSE OF THE TWO AND IT IS NOT FIXED HERE, because converting it means chaining two pickers through peer-resolution logic that follows, and half-doing that is how a working path breaks. NAMED ON THE LIST. *** ALSO CAUGHT, AND IT IS THE SESSION'S RECURRING SHAPE: the driven block first exited 1 WITH ZERO "FAIL" LINES -- closing the jsdom window mid-flight let a page timer fire against a torn-down document. A CRASH IS NOT A FAILED ASSERTION and reads exactly like a clean run to anything counting failures. The window is no longer closed there, with the reason written beside it. READ THE EXIT CODE (v3605, v3681, and now here). CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3691 -- *** SSIM AS WANG ET AL. DEFINE IT, BESIDE THE GLOBAL ONE THAT HAS BEEN HERE SINCE v3337 -- AND THE OBSERVABLE WORTH REPORTING IS NOT THE ONE I BUILT IT FOR. *** teimurjan/blazediff (MIT) ships @blazediff/ssim implementing the published metrics, and looking at it found something better than a dependency: render/perceptual.mjs's ssim() HAS NO EXTERNAL ANCHOR -- grepped for Wang / IEEE / published / literature, ZERO hits -- and it is the GLOBAL form, one window over the whole image, which its own header says honestly. THE PAPER DEFINES A LOCAL MAP: an 11x11 circularly symmetric Gaussian, sigma 1.5, slid over the image, MEAN of the map reported. MEASURED FIRST, WHICH IS WHY THE ROUND EXISTS: invert one corner of a 256x256 render and the global figure reads 0.99872 at 8x8 (0.10% of pixels), 0.99068 at 16x16, 0.97469 at 32x32. A RENDER BUG CONFINED TO ONE REGION IS EXACTLY WHAT RENDER QA IS FOR AND THE NUMBER BARELY MOVES. *** *** AND THE WINDOWED MEAN DOES NOT FIX IT: 0.99810 ON THE SAME 8x8 FIXTURE. I built this expecting the mean to be the answer and it is not -- averaging a local map over an image is still an average over the image. WHAT SEES THE DEFECT IS THE MINIMUM OF THE MAP: -0.7006, AND IT LOCATES IT at (2,0), inside the inverted corner. So ssimWindowed returns the MAP, its MINIMUM and WHERE, not just mssim; reporting the mean alone would have been A NEW NUMBER WITH THE OLD FAILURE INSIDE IT, and a check exists so nobody later "simplifies" the return down to it. A metric that says WHERE turns a red gate into a fixable bug report. *** THE KEYS ARE CLOSED FORMS THE ESTIMATOR IS NEVER TOLD, derived from the published definition rather than from this code: for b = a + d the contrast and structure terms are EXACTLY 1, so SSIM collapses to the luminance term -- matched to 1e-12; for b = k*a the structure term is exactly 1 and the others are exact in k. Self against self is EXACTLY 1, which is arithmetic and not a tolerance. NOTHING IS VENDORED and no blazediff code was read -- ONLY THE PUBLISHED DEFINITION TRAVELLED, the rule heidler's physics and math/elliptic.js's Carlson algorithm came in under. *** *** A PLANT THAT DID NOT FIRE IS THE REASON THE WINDOW IS PINNED AT ALL. Replacing the Gaussian with a BOX window PASSED EVERY CHECK IN THE FILE, because THE CLOSED-FORM KEYS USE FLAT PATCHES and every normalised symmetric window gives the same answer on those -- THOSE KEYS GRADE THE SSIM FORMULA AND NOT THE WINDOW. Added: the edge/centre weight ratio must equal exp(-c^2/2s^2) from sigma alone, 3.866e-3 for c=5 and sigma=1.5, and nothing types it. THE BOX PLANT NOW FIRES. THIRD TIME THIS SESSION THAT A PLANT NOT FIRING WAS THE FINDING. *** REFUSALS THAT MATTER: a window WIDER THAN THE IMAGE throws rather than degrading -- a window covering the whole image IS the global form, and quietly returning that would make this file indistinguishable from the one it exists to be distinguished from, THE WORST POSSIBLE FAILURE HERE (planted; fires). A SIZE MISMATCH throws rather than comparing over the overlap. Edge windows are RENORMALISED rather than dropped, so the result does not depend on image size in a way the definition does not. NOT DONE: nothing consumes this yet -- physics/backendVisualDiff.mjs and render-qa still call the global ssim(), and WHICH CALLERS SHOULD MOVE IS KEITH'S, since the worst-window observable is stricter and would change verdicts. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3690 -- *** A POSITIVE CONTROL IS NOT A PLANT, AND THE PHRASE I WAS USING TO FIND PLANTS FOUND BOTH. *** Working the undeclared binds one at a time after v3688, the search turned up heidler's "exact" mode carrying THE SAME WORDS as gyroscope's sabotage -- "THE LOAD-BEARING NEGATIVE" -- and they are OPPOSITE OBJECTS. gyroscope's sabotage runs the WRONG METHOD and the claim FAILS: precessed 1 -> 0. heidler's exact runs the IDEAL normalisation and the claim becomes PERFECT: peakErrFrac 0.0667 -> 0, which exists to prove the APPARATUS so that the 5% the peak mode reports belongs to the PUBLISHED eta rather than to our arithmetic. DECLARING IT AS A PLANT WOULD HAVE COUNTED A CONTROL AS COVERAGE -- the manufacturing-coverage failure this census exists to prevent, arriving through the very phrase used to look for candidates. TWO THINGS WEARING ONE LABEL, this tree's most repeated shape, and the second time in three rounds that my candidate-finding step was the defect rather than the thing it found. *** THE DISCRIMINATOR IS DIRECTION AND IT IS MECHANICAL: A PLANT MOVES THE CLAIM'S OBSERVABLE AWAY FROM ITS IDEAL; A CONTROL MOVES IT TO THE IDEAL. Both are now declarable, both verified, and REPORTED SEPARATELY. THE PLANT COUNT DID NOT MOVE -- still 31 of 78 -- WHICH IS THE POINT: a subject with a good control and no plant HAS NOT BEEN SHOWN TO CATCH ANYTHING, and folding the two together would have raised a number while lowering what it meant. *** *** THE CONTROL CHECK REFUSES TWO WAYS AND BOTH ARE SHOWN, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each. DECLARE AN IDEAL IT DOES NOT REACH and it names the miss: "peakErrFrac did not reach 0.5 (got 0)". DECLARE AN OBSERVABLE THAT IS ALREADY IDEAL IN THE PRIMARY MODE and it refuses with "was already at the ideal in the primary mode -- proves nothing" -- THE HARDER OF THE TWO AND THE ONE THAT MATTERS, because a control whose observable never had anywhere to travel is a check that cannot fail, and it would otherwise be the easiest kind of coverage to award oneself. A control must REACH the ideal AND START AWAY FROM IT. *** WHAT THIS ROUND DID NOT DO: it did not raise the plant count, and it did not read the other twenty-five candidates. It made the NEXT reading honest -- before this, anything matching "load-bearing negative" would have been declared a plant on sight, and at least one of them is not one. THE CENSUS NOW HAS FOUR PLANT KINDS (mode, method, knob, reader) AND A SEPARATE CONTROL REGISTER, and the two can no longer be added up by accident. STILL UNPARSED: the lbm row, named on every run. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3689 -- *** A FRONT DOOR FOR THE LAPACK KEY, AND A "Mac System" DRAWER THAT IS A VIEW RATHER THAN A SECOND HOME. *** Keith asked for both, and the second one turns on a distinction the machinery makes and the request does not: A CONSOLIDATED PANEL AND A SECOND CLAIM ARE DIFFERENT OPERATIONS. The static `pages` list is an OWNERSHIP claim -- pageSections-selfcheck demands AN ANCHOR FOR THE DRAWER TO MOVE, and AN ANCHOR CAN ONLY BE MOVED TO ONE PLACE, so listing raycast.html under Mac System as well as System Tools would have two drawers fighting over one element and one of them would lose it. That is precisely what "don't remove them from where they are" forbids. SO THE DRAWER CARRIES NO pages AT ALL: it is rendered from macPages() into its own slot, building its own anchors the way the Unsorted drawer does. Nothing moves, nothing is un-claimed. *** MEMBERSHIP IS BY WHAT A PAGE CALLS, NOT WHAT IT MENTIONS, and the first attempt got that wrong: a text scan for "mac|macos|Apple Silicon|Accelerate" returned TEN pages INCLUDING server.html AND predictions.html, which merely DISCUSS a Mac. v3448's prose-mention defect, and the third time this session a name test has been wrong on the first try. The seven are pages that drive a Mac-side route or a .command launcher IN THEIR OWN SCRIPT -- raycast (/raycast/launch, /install/exec), hosting and settings (/hosting/tunnel/status), control, bzflag and rocket-league (.command) -- plus external-linalg.html, Mac-only by construction. EACH CARRIES ITS REASON BESIDE ITS NAME so a later reader can disagree with one entry rather than with the list, and the list is SERVED FROM THE ONE PLACE IT IS DECLARED rather than copied into server.html. *** *** THE PAGE: external-linalg.html shows the state, the graded rows and the four commands. IT CANNOT RUN LAPACK AND SAYS SO IN THOSE WORDS. Nothing in a browser can compile a C file or link Accelerate, and A BUTTON THAT APPEARED TO DO IT WOULD BE THE WORST OBJECT IN THIS SUBJECT -- it would look like corroboration and be a page talking to itself. It READS a grade from a new /linalg/grade route and never re-implements the comparison: the derived tolerance lives in externalLinalg.mjs, and a second copy in a browser would drift the first time either changed. THE ROUTE ANSWERS "absent" HAPPILY, because that is the honest state on every machine where LAPACK has not run and it is not a failure of the route. The table marks the rank rows EXACT INTEGER and the determinant rows with their derived tolerance, because THOSE TWO ROWS ARE NOT WORTH THE SAME and a table that showed them identically would say they were. *** external-linalg.html is CLAIMED by Sampling & Methods, not by Mac System: that drawer is "how you measure ... and their ANSWER KEYS", which is exactly what an outside LAPACK reference is. Mac System lists it as a view. THE OWNERSHIP CLAIM IS THE ONE THAT DECIDES THE ANCHOR, and keeping those two ideas apart is the whole reason this round has a note this long. *** STILL NOT RUN: LAPACK. The page now makes the three commands one copy away instead of one changelog away, but the corroboration DOES NOT EXIST UNTIL A MAC HAS EXECUTED THEM. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3688 -- *** THE FOURTH PLANT SHAPE: A MODE, NOT A KNOB -- AND MY OWN "27 OF 48" FROM LAST ROUND WAS A MENTION TEST, WHICH IS THE ONE THING THIS FILE EXISTS TO REFUSE. *** Keith asked to work the 48 undeclared binds one by one. The second one opened -- gyroscope -- already had its plant, as a MODE you select rather than a flag you set: "sabotage" points the torque ALONG the spin axis instead of across it, which its own header calls THE LOAD-BEARING NEGATIVE. zeta likewise carries with-and-without-corrections. ADDING A `planted` KNOB TO EITHER WOULD HAVE BEEN A SECOND DECLARATION OF ONE IDEA, and doing it 27 times would have manufactured coverage rather than earned it. *** I THEN COUNTED THEM WITH A GREP FOR "sabotage|control|wrong|deliberate" AND REPORTED 27. THAT IS AN UPPER BOUND FROM A TEXT MATCH -- the exact species of claim this file's own header warns about ("A DECLARATION IS NOT A CAPABILITY... several binds DISCUSS planted errors without carrying one"), and the word "wrong" appears in prose across the whole tree. The 27 is not retracted so much as DEMOTED: it is a list worth reading, not a number worth reporting. *** *** SO THE MODE PLANT IS NOW A DECLARATION THAT MUST SAY WHAT IT OVERTURNS, and that requirement is the design. A mode plant CANNOT be verified the way a knob is: building two DIFFERENT modes trivially returns different numbers, so "something moved" proves NOTHING here -- it would certify any bind with two modes. The bind names the observable the plant is supposed to overturn, and the sweep checks THAT ONE changes. gyroscope declares mode "sabotage" flipping `precessed`, MEASURED 1 -> 0: A BINARY THE CLAIM RESTS ON, the same class of evidence as the integer rank v3687 chose for the LAPACK key and the integer agreement v3572 got on 2800 polynomials. *** SHOWN FAILING BOTH WAYS IT CAN LIE, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each: declare an observable the sabotage does NOT flip (`omega`) and the census reports gyroscope as DECLARED BUT DEAD, naming the number that stayed put -- "omega did not move (40 both ways)"; and name a mode that DOES NOT EXIST and it is not counted as a plant at all, rather than being counted and then failing, because A MODE THAT IS NOT IN THE MODE LIST IS NOT A PLANT, it is a typo. *** THE NEW KIND IS KEPT APART FROM THE OLD ONES INSTEAD OF ADDED TO THEM: byKind now reads mode 1, method 6, knob 22, reader 2, and the mode-plant result lives in its own list with its own report line. This file already refused to add knob and reader together on the grounds that THEY ARE NOT THE SAME EVIDENCE; a mode plant is a third thing again, and folding it into the knob totals would have hidden that its verification asks a different question. It is checked BEFORE the knob path so a bind carrying both is never double-counted. VERIFIED 30 -> 31. *** NOT DONE: the other twenty-six candidates from that grep are UNREAD. Each needs the same treatment one at a time -- open it, find whether the mode really is a deliberate wrong method, and name the observable it overturns -- and some will turn out to be prose that merely says "wrong". THE POINT OF THIS ROUND IS THAT THE MACHINERY NOW EXISTS TO DECLARE THEM HONESTLY; the count moves one bind at a time and only when somebody has read it. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3687 -- *** AN ANSWER KEY FROM OUTSIDE THIS ENGINE, FOR THE LINEAR ALGEBRA THE LAB ROLLS BY HAND. *** physics/control/controlStateSpace.mjs computes rank, det, cholesky and solve itself, and ITS OWN HEADER makes the argument this round is built on: "a subject with six independent roads to one number is a subject where a wrong answer has nowhere to hide." EVERY ROAD IT HAS IS ONE WE WROTE. Apple's Accelerate ships LAPACK -- dgesvd, dgetrf -- written by people who have never seen this tree, using different algorithms, tested against different things. That is what an answer key is, and it is the same class of thing as the PUBLISHED ZETA ZEROS the lab already grades against: a number this engine cannot have fitted. *** THE CLAIM WORTH HAVING IS AN INTEGER AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. rank() decides controllability and observability BY ELIMINATION AGAINST A TYPED TOLERANCE (tol = 1e-9); LAPACK decides the same integer FROM THE SINGULAR VALUE SPECTRUM, counting sigma above max(m,n)*eps*sigma1. Those are unrelated derivations of one number. TWO FLOAT ANSWERS AGREEING TO 1e-12 CAN ALWAYS BE AN ACCIDENT OF TOLERANCE; AN INTEGER AGREEING ACROSS TWO UNRELATED METHODS CANNOT. The determinant comparison sits beside it and is worth strictly less. *** *** THE DANGEROUS OBJECT IN THIS ROUND IS THE GRADER, NOT THE REFERENCE, AND IT IS GUARDED FIRST. A grader that returned "pass" when the external answers are missing would certify whatever it was handed FOREVER, ON EVERY MACHINE THAT IS NOT A MAC -- which is every machine these gates run on. It would look exactly like corroboration and be nothing. grade() returns a STATE, never a bare boolean, and ABSENT IS NOT PASS is the first check in the gate. The other checks run against FIXTURE answer files written in the gate itself, because a check that needed LAPACK would skip in precisely the situation it exists to guard. *** SHOWN FAILING, THREE WAYS: an honest reference agrees on all ten problems; a rank off BY ONE is a disagreement with no tolerance to hide in; and an answer file that simply OMITS the hard problems does NOT pass either -- a reference that skips what it found difficult is the same failure as one that gets it wrong, and is the easier of the two to ship by accident. *** THE PROBLEMS ARE THE LAB'S OWN MATRICES, ASSERTED BY IDENTITY: the exported double-integrator.ctrb is byte-equal to what ctrbMatrix() returns, the same call the engine's controllability verdict rests on. INVENTING A TIDY SET WOULD GRADE A PROGRAM NOBODY RUNS. A Hilbert 4x4 is in the set on purpose -- it is where "they agree to 1e-12" becomes a claim about the PROBLEM rather than either solver. The float tolerance is DERIVED from EPS and the problem's size; the integer claims get NO TOLERANCE AT ALL. *** *** THE EXCHANGE FORMAT IS A TRUST DECISION, NOT A CONVENIENCE: problems go out as FLAT TEXT because A JSON PARSER HAND-WRITTEN IN C WOULD BE THE LEAST TRUSTWORTHY LINK IN A CHAIN WHOSE ENTIRE PURPOSE IS TRUST -- a reference that mis-reads its input produces a disagreement INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A REAL ONE. Answers come back as JSON, which printf cannot get wrong. The C side transposes to column-major EXPLICITLY rather than relying on it, because a transpose has the SAME RANK AND THE SAME DETERMINANT and would pass both checks silently. The generated problem files are NOT SHIPPED -- they are regenerable in one command and a stale exported fixture is the launchIndex defect wearing new clothes. *** NOT DONE, AND IT IS HALF THE ROUND: *** LAPACK HAS NOT RUN. *** No Accelerate, no clang and no Apple hardware exist where these gates run, so THE OUTSIDE HALF OF THIS KEY IS UNEXERCISED AND THE CORROBORATION IT PROMISES DOES NOT YET EXIST. Everything shipped here tests the GRADER against fixtures. Three commands on Keith's MacBook turn it into a measurement, and they are in external-linalg.c's header. NOT CLAIMED: any speed benefit. The BLAS underneath will reach the AMX/SME matrix units and that is NOT the reason -- these matrices are 2x2 to 4x4 and the timing is meaningless. THE REASON IS INDEPENDENCE. Nobody should turn this into a benchmark. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3686 -- *** ONE OF THE FORTY-NINE BINDS THAT DECLARED NO PLANTED ERROR NOW HAS ONE, AND IT IS A WRONG DERIVATION SOMEBODY COULD HONESTLY HOLD. *** v3685 left the list worth working one at a time, with the register's own rule: the best plants are where A PLAUSIBLE WRONG DERIVATION EXISTS AND IS TEMPTING. tidal is that case. Geodesic deviation gives +2GM/r^3 ALONG the separation and -GM/r^3 ACROSS it, and THE PLANT DROPS THE FACTOR OF TWO -- using the transverse magnitude for the radial direction, which is precisely what you get by differentiating -GM/r^2 once and forgetting that the reference point is ITSELF FALLING. *** IT IS TEMPTING BECAUSE EVERY SHAPE-OF-THE-ANSWER CHECK IT COULD MEET, IT PASSES: dimensionally right, correct 1/r^3 scaling, still stretches the body (9.07x, measured), still finite and positive, still diverges. A PLANT THAT PRODUCES OBVIOUS NONSENSE PROVES NOTHING -- it would be caught by reading the number rather than by the key. Only the comparison against the EXACT two-geodesic integration separates them, and that comparison IS the key: deviationErrFrac goes 8.88e-4 nominal to 1.58 planted, PAST THE 1e-2 TOLERANCE BY A FACTOR OF ~158, so the tolerance is not what is doing the work. *** THE PLANT REPLACES A LAW, NOT A CONSTANT, which is freeRotationBind's model and the reason that one is worth having: `(planted ? 1 : 2) * M * xi / (r*r*r)`. It reaches every mode that routes through deviationRun -- deviation moves separationLinear and deviationErrFrac, validity moves validitySeparation, validityFrac and both separations. ROCHE DOES NOT MOVE AND THAT IS STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the Roche limit is a different derivation and does not pass through the deviation law, so this knob has nothing to say about it. *** *** THE GATE NOW SHOWS ITS OWN KEY CATCHING THE PLANT rather than asserting that it would. Two checks: the key fires on the halved coefficient AND holds on the nominal one, in the same breath, so a tolerance quietly widened to accommodate anything would fail the second half; and the planted answer is separately shown to STILL LOOK LIKE A TIDAL ANSWER, because a plant nobody could believe is not evidence that a key discriminates. plantedCoverage --verify reads 30 OF 78, up from 29, and the DECLARED-BUT-DEAD LIST STAYS EMPTY -- this one was verified by the sweep that turns the knob and watches, not by declaring it. *** NOT CLAIMED: that the factor of two is the only wrong derivation available here, or that the other forty-eight are as clean. This one was chosen BECAUSE the honest error and the exact reference already sat side by side in the same file; a bind whose only reference is its own arithmetic cannot be planted this way at all, and pretending otherwise would manufacture coverage rather than earn it. FORTY-EIGHT REMAIN. *** AND THE CENSUS GATE CAUGHT MY OMISSION THE MOMENT THE PLANT LANDED: it holds UNDECLARED AT A WALL OF ZERO, and a new plant with no plantKind took it red immediately. Declared `knob` (21 -> 22). A plant nobody can name the kind of is a plant nobody can reason about, and that wall is why the gap was one command rather than a later archaeology round. *** CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3685 -- *** THE INSTRUMENT THAT CERTIFIES PLANTS HAD A FALSE NEGATIVE, AND IT WAS CALLING A WORKING PLANT DEAD. *** plantedCoverage's --verify sweep is the thing that turns a DECLARATION into a MEASUREMENT: 29 of 78 binds SAY they read a planted knob, and the sweep builds each one nominal-against-planted to check the knob actually MOVES a finite observable. Nobody had run it this session. It reported "DECLARED BUT DEAD -- says planted, moves nothing: freerotation". *** THE PLANT IS FINE. THE SWEEP WAS TURNING THE WRONG KNOB. It set `config: { planted: true }`, which is what most binds read -- but freeRotationBind's signature is `build({ mode, config = {}, planted = false })`, WHERE planted IS A SIBLING OF config AND NOT A KEY INSIDE IT. So the sweep built NOMINAL TWICE, saw nothing move, and indicted the bind. TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE KNOB, this tree's most repeated defect, sitting in the census whose entire subject is whether a check would actually fire. *** MEASURED DIRECTLY BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING, which is what separated the two possibilities: with the knob in the place the bind reads, that plant moves sigmaMeasured, sigmaRelErr and sigmaR2 in stability; driftE, driftL, driftWorldL and flips in conserve; and driftWorldL plus attitudeOrder in attitude. IT DELETES THE ENTIRE GYROSCOPIC TERM -- the right-hand side of Euler's equations -- so of course it moves; a plant that removes a law and changes nothing would have been the real story, and it was worth ten seconds to find out which one this was. *** *** A FALSE NEGATIVE IS THE EXPENSIVE DIRECTION HERE and that is why this was worth a round: it sends somebody to repair a plant that already works, it understates the one number this file exists to report, and -- worst -- it teaches you to read "declared but dead" as noise, which is exactly how a GENUINELY dead plant would then survive. 28 -> 29 VERIFIED, and the dead list is now EMPTY: every bind that declares a planted knob has been shown to move something. *** BOTH SPELLINGS ARE TURNED NOW RATHER THAN MAKING 79 DEVICES AGREE ON A PARAMETER SHAPE, which is a different and much larger round. This is a CENSUS OVER HETEROGENEOUS BINDS and its job is to exercise the knob HOWEVER THE BIND SPELLS IT; a bind inert under BOTH is genuinely dead, which is what the report now means. *** SHOWN FAILING IN THE DIRECTION THAT MATTERS, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL: cut the knob's wire in freeRotationBind (`const gyro = true`, ignoring planted) and the sweep goes straight back to 28 and names freerotation. A FIX THAT MADE THE CENSUS BLIND WOULD HAVE PRODUCED THE SAME 29 AS A FIX THAT MADE IT CORRECT, and only this plant separates them. *** ALSO FOUND, NOT FIXED: the census cannot parse the `lbm` row and says so by name (UNPARSED, registered but unreadable), and 49 binds still declare NO planted knob at all -- the list worth working one at a time, with clocks as the model, where a PLAUSIBLE WRONG DERIVATION exists and is tempting. AND ONE OF MINE: I ran the tool from the wrong path first and got MODULE_NOT_FOUND, which reads exactly like a red gate. `find` BEFORE `node`, fifth instance. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3684 -- *** I PAID BACK TWO OF THE HAND-SPELLED CORPUS FILTERS, AND BOTH OF THEM WERE MINE. *** v3614's census named two debts: the twenty tools whose corpus OMITS ALL HTML, and "TWELVE FILES THAT COULD IMPORT moduleRefs' SOURCE_EXT" -- two spellings of one set, which is this tree's most repeated defect. WIDENING THREE OF THE TWENTY (v3678, v3679, v3682) ADDED TO THE SECOND DEBT WHILE PAYING THE FIRST: unboundBuiltin got /\.(js|mjs|html)$/ written out by hand at v3678 and markerSingleSource got a THIRTEENTH spelling at v3682, ONE ROUND AFTER I widened it. A FIX THAT CREATES THE NEIGHBOURING DEFECT IS STILL A DEFECT, and these two are the ones I have no excuse for. Both now import SOURCE_EXT. *** THE SET IS PROVEN UNCHANGED RATHER THAN HOPED: both corpora were CAPTURED BEFORE THE EDIT AND DIFFED AFTER -- 1762 files for unboundBuiltin's eight directories, 3295 for markerSingleSource's whole-tree walk -- BYTE-IDENTICAL BOTH WAYS. A shared constant that quietly matches a DIFFERENT set is worse than the duplicate it replaced, because the duplicate at least fails visibly when somebody edits one copy. *** *** AND THE TRADE A SHARED CONSTANT MAKES IS SHOWN, NOT ASSUMED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: narrow SOURCE_EXT to /\.(js|mjs)$/ and plant a page that declares `const ENGINE_VERSION` -- THE GUARD GOES QUIET. Zero failures, the second declaration UNSEEN, exactly the defect v3682 was built to catch. Restore the constant and the same planted page fires immediately, naming both files. ONE CONSTANT NOW DISARMS EVERY CONSUMER AT ONCE, which is the price of one declaration and is worth paying only because the alternative is thirteen copies drifting apart in silence -- but it is a price, and nobody should meet it by surprise. *** THIS ROUND ALSO REFUTED ITS OWN FIRST TWO HYPOTHESES, BOTH BY MEASUREMENT BEFORE ANY EDIT. I expected the "15 UNREFERENCED SHADER FILES" figure to be wrong because 27 pages name a shader and shaderCensus walks .js/.mjs only -- BUT shaderRefs, WHICH IS WHAT PRODUCES THAT NUMBER, ALREADY SCANS .html. The number stands. And shaderCensus itself turned out not to be a reference census at all: it pairs GLSL against WGSL (3 both, 11 wgsl-only, 132 glsl-only), so its corpus omission is a different question entirely. TWO WRONG TARGETS, KILLED BY READING THE TOOL RATHER THAN ITS NAME -- which is cheaper than a round spent fixing the wrong thing, and is the same lesson as the last two rounds in a new costume. *** NOT DONE: ten or more hand-spellings remain, and they are NOT swept. v3202 rewrote a class of call sites in one script and deleted 61 live modules; the two here were converted individually with a set-equality proof each, and the next ten deserve the same. SEVENTEEN OF THE TWENTY CORPUS TOOLS ALSO REMAIN. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3683 -- *** THE PROSE-DEBT CLASSIFIER TREATED UNKNOWN AS GUILTY, AND THREE OF ITS FORTY-THREE ENTRIES WERE NEVER SCANNING A FILE AT ALL. *** The line read `!siteUnwraps && (!arg || fromFile.has(...))`, so when the identifier pattern failed to match -- because the argument is a CALL, an await, or a regex kept in a table and applied somewhere else -- THE SITE WAS COUNTED AS SCANNING SOURCE. v3103's law, inverted, sitting in the classifier that decides the whole list's membership. The third state is NAMED AND PRINTED BY COUNT on every run rather than folded into either answer. 43 -> 40, AND THE NAMED LIST WAS RE-FROZEN IN THE SAME ROUND, because a list that narrows while its number stays put is a ratchet with slack (v3672, v3673). *** I ALMOST SHIPPED THAT SLACK WITH A CHANGELOG SAYING I HAD NOT. I wrote an edit to lower `const BASELINE = 43` -- A LINE THAT DOES NOT EXIST: the baseline is DERIVED, `BASELINE_LIST.length`, from the frozen JSON. The python assertion failed, nothing changed, AND I MISREAD THAT AssertionError AS COMING FROM THE PLANT IN THE SAME COMMAND. The build read "40 (baseline 43)" -- passing, with three slots of slack -- and only re-running from the EXTRACT and reading the number surfaced it. THE ROUND-TRIP AND THE GATE WERE BOTH GREEN; what caught it was reading the reported figure against what the changelog claimed. *** EACH OF THE THREE WAS READ, NOT RULED ON. actionCache tests `.why`, A STRING RETURNED BY A FUNCTION. claimsGate applies its pattern as pat.test(m) to a JUDGED MESSAGE. syncWalkProbe's entry IS NOT A REGEX AT ALL -- it is the two slashes inside the check NAME "the cold/warm split and the readdir/stat split", lexed as a literal because this scan keeps STRINGS. A RE-WRAPPED COMMENT CANNOT BE THE FAILURE MODE OF ANY OF THE THREE, which is the only thing this list means. *** *** AND I TRIED TO SPLIT THE BUCKET IN TWO AND DROPPED IT, WHICH IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF OF THE ROUND. Having found that syncWalkProbe is a phantom, I wanted "not a regex at all" as its own state, and reached for the discriminator that has worked twice -- does the literal survive codeOnly. IT CALLED TWELVE SITES PHANTOMS, INCLUDING claimsGate's, WHICH IS A REAL REGEX. A classifier wrong about eleven of twelve is not a finding, it is a guess with a count attached, and the count it produced (36) was LOWER and would have looked like more progress. NOT SHIPPED. The three are excused on the one statement that is true of all of them either way: THIS PARSER CANNOT READ WHAT THEY APPLY THE PATTERN TO. The phantom is named in a comment instead, where a wrong classifier cannot make it a number. *** THE ROUND BEFORE THIS ONE ENDED WITH NOTHING SHIPPED because an edit half-applied -- a python anchor matched nothing, the assertion failed, and only a dead reporting block landed, so the bucket read empty and I briefly believed the defect was not real. THIS TIME EVERY EDIT WAS CONFIRMED BY GREP AFTER APPLYING, not by the absence of an error. Two of my earlier five "unreadable" sites were an artifact of MY OWN reconstruction: the baseline stores each regex TRUNCATED AT 52 CHARS, so rebuilding the pattern from it cannot match the source. RUN THE REAL THING, NOT YOUR MODEL OF IT -- the gate's own extraction returns NULL for actionCache, and that is what settled it. *** SHOWN FAILING: put a raw-source prose regex back into launchIndex-selfcheck and the gate names it as a NEW offender, so narrowing the unknown did not blunt the check it belongs to. NOT CLAIMED: that the remaining 40 are all real debt. v3106 measured 28 of 38 attempted conversions BREAK; THE NUMBER IS A CEILING ON DEBT, NOT A MEASURE OF IT, and it is now a smaller ceiling because three things that were never on the wrong side of it came off. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3682 -- *** THE THIRD OF v3614's TWENTY, AND THIS ONE IS THE GUARD ON THE VERSION MARKER ITSELF. *** markerSingleSource-selfcheck exists because A VERSION MARKER IN TWO FILES IS THE WORST KIND OF SECOND DECLARATION -- THE COPY THAT DRIFTS IS THE ONE THAT TELLS YOU WHAT BUILD YOU ARE LOOKING AT -- and it was scanning .js and .mjs only. A PAGE HARDCODING `const ENGINE_VERSION = "v3681"` WOULD HAVE BEEN INVISIBLE TO THE ONE CHECK WRITTEN TO CATCH EXACTLY THAT, across 379 pages carrying 517 inline scripts. *** MEASURED BEFORE WIDENING, WHICH IS NOW THE RULE FOR THIS LIST: three pages mention the markers and NOT ONE DECLARES ONE. cosmic-map READS window.ENGINE_VERSION, predictions names it in PROSE inside a string, and ship.html uses it as a TABLE LABEL in a template literal. ZERO FALSE POSITIVES WERE AVAILABLE, so the widening costs no noise and buys the guard -- the same answer v3678 and v3679 reached by the same method, and the reason the alternative (a stated reason) was not taken. *** *** AND THE EXISTING DETECTION ALREADY HANDLED PAGES WITHOUT A SINGLE CHANGE, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING: the idiom is LINE-ANCHORED and read through codeOnly. ship.html's label sits inside a template literal, which codeOnly blanks; predictions' mention sits in a string, likewise. THOSE TWO DECISIONS WERE MADE AT v3455 AND v3456 FOR A DIFFERENT REASON ENTIRELY -- eight files were counting as declarations because noComments keeps strings, and five regexes READING the marker were counting because they sit mid-line after match( -- and they are exactly what makes a page safe to scan today. A CORRECT DISCRIMINATOR PAYS TWICE. Only <script> BODIES are read from a page, never the markup, because prose or an attribute naming a marker is not a declaration of one. *** SHOWN FAILING, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL: hardcode the marker into an inline script and the gate names BOTH FILES -- "declared in 2 files: cosmic-map.html, main.js" -- which is the failure that could not have been reported yesterday. AND THE CONTROL RUNS BESIDE IT: ship.html's table label STILL does not count, so the widening did not buy its guard by loosening the idiom. *** NO VERDICT MOVED: BRAIN_BUILD is still declared once in brain/brain.js and ENGINE_VERSION once in main.js. THE OTHER HALF OF THIS GATE IS UNTOUCHED AND STILL PASSING -- WebGLEngine/brain.js, the stale duplicate that carried a marker 270 versions behind, is gone AND its recovery archive is still in tools/ship/deleted/, which is Keith's rule that a deletion without an archive cost 43 versions once. SEVENTEEN TOOLS REMAIN. Each is its own judgement and three have now been widened BECAUSE THE COUNT SAID SO; the next may honestly want a stated reason instead, and DO NOT SWEEP (v3202 rewrote a class of call sites in one script and deleted 61 live modules). *** ALSO THIS ROUND, NOT IN THE TREE: my handoff notes were at their size cap, so v3668-v3681 were consolidated into one block of lessons -- a file nobody can add to is a file that stops recording. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3681 -- *** 101 FILES IMPORT THIS TREE'S ANSWER TO PROSE-AS-CODE AND NOTHING HAD EVER DRIVEN IT. *** Twice in four rounds a detector counted its own test fixture -- v3677 gateQuality on its sabotage plant, v3680 deadImportScan on moduleRefs' worker string -- and BOTH WERE FOUND BY ACCIDENT. Going looking for the class rather than the third instance found something better: sourceScan.mjs, whose codeOnly and noComments are the answer every one of those twenty-plus prose-as-code findings routes through, HAD NO GATE OF ITS OWN. A STRIPPER THAT SILENTLY STOPPED STRIPPING WOULD TURN ALL 101 CHECKS INTO PROSE MATCHING AND NOTHING WOULD GO RED -- they would simply start passing for the wrong reason. NEW tools/ship/sourceScan-selfcheck.mjs, 15 checks. *** AND THE CENSUS FOUND THAT FAILURE ALREADY SHIPPED, IN A SECOND COPY. tools/ship/unboundBuiltin.mjs DECLARED ITS OWN codeOnly, and its docstring promised "Strip comments and string literals: prose that mentions os.homedir() is not a use of it" -- AND `// os.homedir()` SURVIVED IT INTACT. THE ONE THING IT PROMISED WAS THE ONE THING IT DID NOT DO: prose-as-code inside the guard written to prevent prose-as-code. *** MEASURED, NOT ARGUED: run both strippers over every .js and .mjs and THEY DISAGREE ON 2794 OF 2910 FILES, and TEN name-uses in the live tree are seen ONLY by the private one -- every one from a line comment (deadImportScan.mjs:fs, ev.html:fs, proseAudit.mjs:fs and seven more). NONE reaches a finding today, but only because isBound happens to rescue them from the RAW source: A SECOND ACCIDENT HOLDING UP THE FIRST. deadImportScan's own header already says why this must not happen -- "a fix applied to one tool is not a fix to the class; sourceScan.mjs exists to be the single definition" -- and that sentence was written while a second definition sat two files away. *** *** THE PRIVATE COPY IS GONE, IMPORTED AND RE-EXPORTED SO THE ONE CONSUMER OF THE NAME KEEPS WORKING AGAINST ONE DECLARATION, AND THE FINDINGS ARE IDENTICAL AFTER THE SWAP: the single ui/kpopFavorites.js `url` hit verify has always carried as a NAMED exception, unchanged. A BARE `export ... from` DOES NOT BIND THE NAME LOCALLY, so scan()'s own call threw ReferenceError -- and THE GATE EXITED 1 WHILE PRINTING ZERO "FAIL" LINES, because a crash is not a failed assertion. Read the exit code, not the count (v3605, third time these notes have recorded it). *** AND I HAND-SPELLED AN EXPORTED HELPER TWICE THIS SESSION WITHOUT NOTICING. codeHas(src, re) IS `re.test(codeOnly(src))` and has been exported all along; I wrote that expression by hand in gateQuality at v3677 and again in deadImportScan at v3680, each time as though it were new, IN THE TWO FILES WHOSE SUBJECT IS SECOND SPELLINGS OF ONE IDEA. The gate now asserts the equivalence so the two forms cannot drift, and asserts the thing both rounds actually needed: A CALL NESTED INSIDE A STRING DISAPPEARS WITH ITS HOST UNDER codeOnly WHILE A REAL ONE SURVIVES WITH A BLANKED ARGUMENT, which is how a gate stops counting its own fixture. *** WHAT THE GATE ASSERTS AND WHY EACH IS SEPARATE: codeOnly blanks line comments, block comments and string CONTENTS while KEEPING THE QUOTES (that last is what v3680 rests on) and leaves the code intact; noComments KEEPS strings, because a module specifier IS a string literal and codeOnly would blank the thing the reference graph hunts; prose() answers the opposite question and the three are NOT interchangeable. The second declaration's absence is asserted BY MECHANISM. The line-comment claim is driven ON A FIXTURE AND THEN OVER THE WHOLE TREE -- 2911 files -- because THE PRIVATE COPY PASSED EVERY FIXTURE ANYBODY WROTE FOR IT AND STILL FAILED THERE. *** SHOWN FAILING, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each: disable codeOnly's line-comment branch -- THE EXACT BUG THIS ROUND REMOVED -- and 2 checks go red including the tree walk; give unboundBuiltin a private stripper back and the second-declaration check goes red. MY FIRST ATTEMPT AT THE FIRST PLANT MATCHED NOTHING AND THE GATE PASSED, which would have read as a plant that does not fire; the anchor was wrong, and it was re-applied by line rather than believed. A KNOWN LIMIT IS CARRIED FORWARD RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPED: the lexer desyncs on a regex literal containing a quote, and the gate checks that sourceScan ITSELF still declares that, since a limit recorded only in a gate is one the file's own readers never meet. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3680 -- *** THE LAST CARRIED RED WAS A TEST'S PROP COUNTED AS A BROKEN SPAWN, AND IT HAD BEEN RED SINCE v3478. *** deadImportScan-selfcheck read "6 spawn sites, 2 broken, 1 UNEXPLAINED" and I went in expecting the unexplained one to be the missing terrain worker the notes have carried for two hundred versions. IT IS NOT. That one is BASELINED, with a reason so thorough it is worth restating: v3532 records that its FIRST reason was wrong for 53 versions and sent a round through FOURTEEN ZIPS hunting a file nobody lost -- wasm-terrain/README.md lists world/terrainGenWorker.js in its own layout AS SOURCE, so it is a lost source file, absent from every archive reachable here, and restoring it alone would fix nothing because it imports world/wasm/terrain_wasm.js. THE ENTRY IS SOUND AND THE GATE PROVES IT IS STILL FIRING. *** THE UNEXPLAINED ONE WAS tools/ship/moduleRefs-selfcheck.mjs -> ./worker.js, AND THERE IS NO SPAWN THERE AT ALL: it is the FIXTURE for that gate's own resolver test, the string 'const wk = new Worker("./worker.js");' built as a prop. THE DETECTOR WAS COUNTING A TEST'S PROP AS A BROKEN SPAWN -- the defect landing on the file studying it, the same shape v3677 found when gateQuality counted its own sabotage fixture, and the second time in four rounds. *** *** THE CAUSE IS A CORRECT DECISION WITH AN UNSTATED COST. workerSpecifiers reads noComments and NOT codeOnly, deliberately: A SPECIFIER IS A STRING LITERAL, so blanking strings would blank the thing being hunted. What nobody had written down is that this leaves it unable to tell a real `new Worker("./x.js")` from THE SAME TEXT QUOTED INSIDE ANOTHER STRING. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS MECHANICAL AND NEEDS NO LIST: codeOnly blanks string CONTENTS while keeping the code around them, so a REAL spawn survives it as `new Worker("")` while a spawn nested inside a string DISAPPEARS WITH ITS HOST. A file with no surviving spawn call has only fixtures. *** THE LIMIT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: the rule is FILE-scoped, so a file holding BOTH a real spawn and a fixture would keep both. ALL SIX SPAWN SITES WERE CHECKED -- five real, one all-fixture, NONE MIXED -- so no such file exists today, and if one appears this UNDER-REPORTS rather than inventing a spawn, which is the safe direction for a check whose whole subject is a claim about the outside world. Not a list of blessed files: the same code answers for any file that ever gets one. *** SHOWN FAILING IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each, because a rule that hides a fixture could just as easily hide everything: plant a REAL `new Worker("./ghostWorker.js")` and the gate goes red at 2 broken and 1 unexplained; add a SECOND fixture string to the gate and it stays green at 5 sites and 1 broken. THE FIRST PLANT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS -- it proves the detector still sees what it is for. *** THE READING IS NOW 5 SPAWN SITES, 1 BROKEN, 0 UNEXPLAINED, and the one broken is the one with a written reason that the gate independently confirms is STILL BROKEN, so the suppression cannot outlive it. CARRIED REDS: NONE, AND THE BY-HAND SET IS EIGHT: pageReach, gateQuality, pageSections, deviceInstrumentMap, graveyard, orphanTriage, launchIndex, deadImportScan. NOT CLAIMED: that terrainGenWorker.js is recoverable. Nothing in this round looked for it again; v3532 already established it is not here, and re-running a hopeless hunt is the fabricated-provenance mistake in a different costume. // v3679 -- *** THE SECOND OF v3614's TWENTY, AND THIS ONE MATTERED MOST BECAUSE ITS CORPUS OMITTED THE CASE WHERE THE IMPORT ACTUALLY RUNS. *** tools/ship/deadImportScan.mjs asks "does this import name a file that exists" and walked .js and .mjs only. ITS FOUNDING STORY (v3126) IS A BARREL THAT WOULD THROW ON ITS FIRST IMPORT AND NEVER DID, BECAUSE NOTHING LOADED IT -- and a PAGE is the other case: 216 OF 379 PAGES CARRY MODULE IMPORTS, 620 SITES, and every one of them runs the moment somebody opens the page. Not one was scanned. MEASURED FIRST, AS v3678 ESTABLISHED: all 620 resolve, so the widening buys a guard and costs no noise. *** *** IT IS A SEPARATE FUNCTION RATHER THAN A WIDER SPEC, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING CHOICE. Two things differ on a page: THE SPECIFIERS ARE ABSOLUTE (SPEC admits only ones beginning with `.`, and resolves() resolves relative to the importing file, so "/physics/livePanel.js" would reach the MACHINE ROOT rather than the served directory); and ONLY <script> BODIES ARE CODE. Widening SPEC and resolves would have moved EVERY OTHER CONSUMER OF THEM -- exportResolve, markerSingleSource, moduleRefs -- for a reason that has nothing to do with those tools. A ROUND MUST NOT MOVE A VERDICT IT IS NOT ABOUT, and the separation is CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSERTED: all three re-run with their readings unchanged against a pristine v3678 extract. *** AND THE SPECIFIER SHAPE WAS THE DISCRIMINATOR, LEARNED THE HARD WAY. My first extractor matched `import` after any non-word character and accepted a specifier starting with `[./]` -- and pulled `...VoxelWorld.js` out of a PROSE STRING in predictions.html, reporting a broken import that does not exist. A page is mostly prose and this file's whole subject is a claim about the outside world, so reading that prose as imports would INVENT BREAKAGES NOBODY CAN FIX. A REAL SPECIFIER BEGINS `/`, `./` OR `../` -- THREE DOTS IS NOT A PATH -- and that alone removes it. Prose-as-code, caught before it was believed, and both halves of the fix are driven in the gate rather than described: the markup stripper is fed a page fragment and must return only the script, and the three-dot specifier must resolve to nothing. *** SHOWN FAILING: point live-panel.html at /physics/livePanelGONE.js and the gate names THE PAGE AND THE SPECIFIER; tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL. *** *** AND RUNNING THIS GATE AT ALL FOUND THAT IT WAS ALREADY RED, WHICH IS THE THIRD TIME THIS SESSION THAT A GATE I WAS NOT RUNNING BY HAND WAS FAILING WITHOUT ME REPORTING IT. deadImportScan-selfcheck reads "6 spawn sites, 2 broken, 1 unexplained" and is BYTE-IDENTICAL ON A PRISTINE v3678 EXTRACT, so it is not this round's. It is the world/terrainGenWorker.js absence the notes have carried since v3478 -- A WORKER TARGET IS NOT AN IMPORT, the file is genuinely gone, and the fix is Keith's archives or a decision rather than a bug hunt. verify does NOT drive this gate, which is exactly how it stayed invisible; IT IS ON THE BY-HAND LIST NOW. NOT CLAIMED: that the other eighteen tools should be widened. This one could be BECAUSE THE COUNT SAID SO -- 620 sites, zero broken -- and the next may honestly want a stated reason instead. DO NOT SWEEP (v3202). CARRIED REDS: deadImportScan-selfcheck 1, pre-existing since v3478, not mine, named above. // v3678 -- *** ONE OF THE TWENTY TOOLS SCANNING A CORPUS THAT OMITS EVERY HTML FILE, WIDENED RATHER THAN EXCUSED -- AND WIDENING IT WALKED STRAIGHT ONTO A HOLE THAT WAS ALWAYS THERE. *** v3614 found 155 filter sites over 33 corpora and named the group that matters: TWENTY tools whose filter admits .js and .mjs and SILENTLY OMITS ALL 384 HTML, so any count from them is not comparable with a SOURCE_EXT tool and nothing said so. The open list has asked each for A WIDENED FILTER OR A STATED REASON. This is the first one taken, and it was chosen BY CONSEQUENCE rather than by list order: tools/ship/unboundBuiltin.mjs is DRIVEN BY verify EVERY SHIP, so its rule was unenforced across 517 inline scripts in 377 pages. *** I EXPECTED TO WRITE A REASON AND THE MEASUREMENT SAID WIDEN. The reason looked solid: `crypto` and `url` are legitimate BROWSER globals, so the rule should not transfer and would flag correct pages -- and this file's own header says A DETECTOR THAT FLAGS EVERYTHING IS WORSE THAN NONE. MEASURED ACROSS ALL 379 PAGES BEFORE TOUCHING ANYTHING: NOT ONE of the ten names is used as a namespace anywhere, so there were ZERO false positives available and the widening costs no noise at all. A REASON I BELIEVED, REFUTED BY THE COUNT IT RESTED ON. *** *** AND IT IS THE BROWSER HALF OF A DEFECT THIS TREE HAS HIT FOUR TIMES: v3617, v3630, v3669 and v3674 were all "a module a browser cannot load", each a node: import at module scope. THIS IS THE INLINE-SCRIPT VERSION -- a page reaching for fs or path crashes the moment it opens, on every platform, with nothing to catch it -- and until now no check could see it. ONLY <script> BODIES ARE READ FROM A PAGE, never the markup: prose or an attribute containing "fs." is not a use of fs, and scanning raw HTML would be the prose-as-code mistake recorded twenty-plus times here. *** THE HOLE THE WIDENING FOUND, AND IT IS THE BEST PART: the first widened run reported cosmic-map.html using `path` unbound. IT IS A LOCAL VARIABLE -- a route, `path.length`, `path.forEach` -- declared as `let seed = 42, G, adj, from = -1, to = -1, path = null;`. isBound demanded the name IMMEDIATELY AFTER const/let/var, SO ANY NAME DECLARED LATER IN A MULTI-DECLARATOR LIST READ AS UNBOUND. That hole has been in the detector since v3033 and applies to .js and .mjs exactly as much; NOTHING HAD STOOD ON IT, and widening the corpus is what walked onto it. THE PAGE IS CORRECT AND THE DETECTOR WAS WRONG, so the detector is what changed. My first repair excluded `=` from the run-on prefix, which cannot match a list whose earlier declarators have initialisers -- caught by re-running rather than by reading. *** VERIFIED THE WAY THIS ROUND HAS TO BE: the page root reports ZERO, and every directory verify already scanned reports exactly what it did before -- the single ui/kpopFavorites.js `url` hit that verify has carried as a NAMED exception all along. NO EXISTING VERDICT MOVED. "." is added to verify's scan list, so the guard is live every ship. SHOWN FAILING: plant `const boot = fs.readFileSync("x")` into an inline script and it is caught and NAMED, cosmic-map.html:fs; tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL. NOT DONE: the other nineteen. Each is its own judgement and this one took a measurement to settle, which is the argument against sweeping them (v3202 deleted 61 live modules with one script). NOT CLAIMED: that the remaining nineteen should all be widened -- this one could be BECAUSE THE COUNT SAID SO, and the next may honestly want a reason instead. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3677 -- *** THE LIMIT I NAMED AT THE CHECK LAST ROUND, CLOSED -- AND MY OWN FIX HAD THE SAME BUG IN MINIATURE, WHICH THE PLANT CAUGHT. *** v3676 stopped a COMMENT from satisfying gateQuality's unwrap test and wrote down what it left behind: the test was still FILE-scoped, so ONE genuine unwrap anywhere in a gate exempted EVERY prose regex in it and a half-converted file read as converted. This asks the question where it belongs -- OF THIS REGEX'S OWN .test() ARGUMENT. *** AND MY FIRST VERSION TOOK A FIXED 160-CHARACTER WINDOW AFTER `.test(`, with a comment claiming it was "short enough not to run into the next statement". THAT IS FALSE THE MOMENT TWO CHECKS SHARE A LINE, and this tree writes `/A/.test(x) && /B/.test(noComments(x))` all the time: the window reached the SIBLING call, so a wrapped neighbour cleared a raw site. THE FILE-SCOPE BUG I WAS REMOVING, SHRUNK TO A LINE, WITH A SENTENCE ASSERTING IT COULD NOT HAPPEN. Caught because the plant -- half-convert a file and see it fail -- DID NOT FIRE. A PLANT THAT DOES NOT FIRE IS A FINDING, second time this session. The argument is bounded by ITS OWN MATCHING CLOSE PAREN now (argOf walks and counts), so the text tested is exactly this call's argument and nothing beyond it; a fixed window either truncates a nested call or reaches the next one, and BOTH failures read as a verdict rather than as an error. *** *** THE COUNT WENT 19 -> 43 AND IT WENT UP BECAUSE THE DETECTOR GOT HONEST, NOT BECAUSE DEBT ARRIVED. Twenty-four sites had been exempt for as long as their gate happened to call an unwrapper ANYWHERE in itself, and every one of them is in a file NOTHING TOUCHED THIS SESSION: debt MADE VISIBLE, not debt created. THEY ARE FROZEN INTO THE NAMED LIST RATHER THAN CONVERTED, and the precedent is this file's own v3139, which froze from what was actually there for exactly this reason -- A NAMED LIST SAYS WHICH ENTRIES ARE OWED WHERE A RAISED COUNT RATIFIES THEM SILENTLY. Converting twenty-four in one pass is v3090's per-file sweep, which is how thirty-two conversions went back; and v3106 MEASURED that 28 of 38 attempted conversions BREAK, because a regex that breaks when comments are the only thing left WAS NEVER HUNTING A COMMENT. THE LIST MAY ONLY SHRINK, and it is a HARDER list to be on than it was yesterday: an entry can no longer be cleared by a sibling site in the same file, or by a sibling call on the same line. *** AND THE DETECTOR WAS COUNTING ITS OWN SABOTAGE FIXTURE. gateQuality-selfcheck carries a PLANTED prose regex as the fixture for its own "a long contiguous prose regex IS detected" check, and that plant became an offender the instant the file-level exemption came off -- it had only ever been exempt BECAUSE ITS OWN CODE CALLS noComments(. THE DEFECT LANDING ON THE FILE STUDYING IT, seventh-plus instance, and the standing antidote applies: excluded BY IDENTITY from import.meta.url, never by name, so a rename cannot silently re-admit it. The sabotage check still passes, because it drives the fixture directly rather than by scanning the file. *** SHOWN FAILING BOTH WAYS, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each: half-convert a file -- one site raw, its sibling wrapped, ON THE SAME LINE -- and the gate now names that exact site (it did NOT before the paren fix, which is how the flaw was found); and v3676's comment trick still moves the census NOT AT ALL. NOT CLAIMED: that the 43 are all real debt. THE NUMBER IS STILL A CEILING ON DEBT, NOT A MEASURE OF IT, and it is now a more honest ceiling rather than a smaller one -- which is the opposite of what a round is usually tempted to report. CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3676 -- *** THE LAST CARRIED RED IS GREEN, AND THE CHECK THAT HUNTS PROSE-AS-CODE WAS ITSELF SATISFIED BY PROSE. *** gateQuality has been red for many rounds and my notes said it was NOT A ROUND. Reading it rather than trusting that: the failure printed "NEW offender(s): unwrap the comment first ... or use noComments()" AND NEVER SAID WHICH. Its own header records why the check was rewritten from a count to a NAMED comparison -- "it could say THREE TOO MANY AND NOT WHICH THREE" -- THE COMPARISON WAS FIXED AND THE MESSAGE WAS NOT, so the red stayed unactionable for exactly the reason the rewrite was meant to remove. A NAMED COMPARISON THAT REPORTS A NUMBER IS STILL A COUNT. The message now derives from the SAME set difference the condition uses, and names arrivals AND departures. *** ITS SIBLING LIED IN THE OTHER DIRECTION: "the baseline is honest" chose its text with a two-branch ternary, below-or-at, so ABOVE THE BASELINE PRINTED "at baseline" -- A FAILING LINE THAT READS LIKE A PASSING ONE, on the check whose whole job is saying whether the baseline is honest. Third case added. AND MY OWN DIFF-AGAINST-PRISTINE DISCIPLINE COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT ANY OF THIS: I have reported this red as "byte-identical to pristine, not mine" for four rounds, and the line I was diffing COULD NOT DISTINGUISH WHICH OFFENDERS IT MEANT. Comparing an uninformative message is not corroboration. *** *** WITH THE NAMES VISIBLE THE ROUND CHANGED SHAPE. The two newcomers were in launchIndex-selfcheck, and running that gate -- WHICH I HAD NOT BEEN RUNNING BY HAND -- found it ALREADY RED on something else entirely: launch-index.json was STALE, 453 entries shipped against 482 computed. v3610's finding, again, and the artefact rebuild is the whole fix. SECOND TIME IN FOUR ROUNDS THAT RUNNING SOMETHING I HAD NOT BEEN RUNNING FOUND A RED I WAS NOT REPORTING. *** THEN THE DETECTOR ITSELF. Its exemption test -- does this gate unwrap before matching -- READ RAW SOURCE, so a COMMENT saying noComments( COUNTED AS THE FIX. SHOWN, NOT ARGUED: adding one comment line to actionCache-selfcheck.mjs dropped the census 19 -> 18 with no code changed at all. PROSE-AS-CODE, IN THE DETECTOR WHOSE ENTIRE SUBJECT IS PROSE-AS-CODE -- the defect landing on the file studying it, sixth-plus instance, and this one had been letting real debt out for as long as anybody had written the word in a comment. The test reads codeOnly(src) now, which is the documented stripper for an IDIOM and blanks comments AND strings so neither can satisfy it. *** *** AND FIXING IT IMMEDIATELY EXPOSED TWO REAL OFFENDERS IT HAD BEEN HIDING: terrainBuild-selfcheck.mjs, exempt because that file mentions noComments( in a COMMENT ONLY. Both are now routed through noComments, and WHICH STRIPPER WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN GUESSED: both phrases are REAL USER-FACING STRINGS in terrainBuildBridge.js (the Windows PATH note at line 102, the refusal at line 110), so noComments is right -- it keeps STRINGS and drops COMMENTS -- where codeOnly would have blanked the very text they assert. The same question was asked and answered the same way for launchIndex's pair, at lines 243 and 249 of page-index.html. *** THE DETECTOR IS STRICTLY STRICTER AND THE COUNT IS UNCHANGED AT 19 OF 19, which is the honest outcome: two hidden offenders came in and two were paid off, and the baseline was NOT touched in either direction. SHOWN FAILING BOTH WAYS: the comment trick that worked ten minutes earlier now moves the census NOT AT ALL; and before the fix, the same trick moved it. *** A KNOWN LIMIT IS STATED RATHER THAN QUIETLY LEFT: the exemption is still FILE-scoped, so ONE genuine unwrap anywhere in a gate exempts every prose regex in it and a half-converted file reads as converted. Narrowing it to the SITE would re-admit every partly-converted file at once and is a different, larger round; it is named at the check so the next reader meets the limit instead of rediscovering it. NOT CLAIMED: that the remaining 19 are all real debt -- v3106 measured that 28 of 38 attempted conversions BREAK, because a regex that breaks when comments are the only thing left was never hunting a comment. THE NUMBER IS STILL A CEILING ON DEBT, NOT A MEASURE OF IT. *** CARRIED REDS: NONE. // v3675 -- *** THE LAST RATCHET I OWNED WAS FIRING ON CORRECT WORK, AND THE PROOF IS A THREE-LINE MODULE. *** graveyard's TOTAL has read red for many rounds and v3673 left it red on purpose, saying the real fix was its own round. THIS IS THAT ROUND, and the finding is measured rather than argued: ADD A LEGITIMATE ANALYSIS PRIMITIVE -- a pure module carrying its own MEASURED_ record, driven by its own gate, THE EXACT THING THIS PROJECT BUILDS MOST ROUNDS -- AND THE TOTAL GOES 143 -> 144 WHILE THE ACTIONABLE COUNT STAYS AT 86 AND NO DEBT EXISTS ANYWHERE IN THE TREE. v3567's law, arriving in a ratchet instead of a key: A CHECK THAT FIRES ON CORRECT CODE IS WORSE THAN NO CHECK. *** THE HISTORY TELLS THE SAME STORY SLOWLY AND IT IS WHY THE CONSTANT IS DELETED RATHER THAN LOWERED: it was 8, RAISED TO 14 at v3223, RAISED TO 117 at v3451, each raise honest and each the same admission -- the number goes up when the project succeeds. A CONSTANT THAT MUST BE RAISED EVERY TIME THE WORK SUCCEEDS IS NOT A BASELINE. It is REMOVED rather than left declared-and-unused, because a dead ceiling in the source reads as load-bearing to the next person, who would raise it again; its history is kept in the comment where the evidence belongs. *** *** WHAT REPLACES IT IS STRICTLY STRONGER AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT, BECAUSE CLOSING A RED BY DELETING AN ASSERTION IS THE MOVE THIS TREE REFUSES. A CEILING CAN BE MET BY RAISING A NUMBER; A PARTITION CANNOT. Every gate-only module must now fall into EXACTLY ONE of four named populations -- analysis records, orphaned utilities, modules with a tools.html door (v3673), and entries explained with a stated blocker -- and the check goes red the moment a member belongs to none, which is the failure a total nobody reads would hide: an unclassified gate-only module. 143 classified of 143. THE COUNT THAT MEANS SOMETHING IS UNTOUCHED AND STILL RATCHETED at 86. *** BOTH HALVES SHOWN FAILING, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL after each: drop one population from the sum and the partition goes RED; and -- the one that matters, because it is the accusation this round invites -- ADD A GENUINELY UNWIRED MODULE WITH A GATE AND THE ORPHAN RATCHET STILL BITES, 86 -> 87 red, WHILE THE PARTITION CORRECTLY STAYS GREEN AT 144 OF 144. THE TWO CHECKS ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND BOTH STILL WORK: one asks whether debt arrived, the other whether anything went unclassified. Retiring the ceiling took neither away. *** NOT CLAIMED: that the four populations are the right four forever. The antidote is written at the check -- if a FIFTH is ever needed, ADD IT AND NAME IT, and DO NOT widen an existing category to absorb the stragglers, because the whole value of the line is that every member was put somewhere on purpose. NOT DONE: the five still-undecided libraries (coverage, routeContrast, skillTrial, suspiciousZero, trendVsNoise) are inside the 86 and remain a judgement per module. CARRIED REDS: gateQuality 2 ONLY -- diffed against a pristine extract and not mine. graveyard IS GREEN. // v3674 -- *** TWO MODULES THAT WERE ONE FEATURE, BOTH PROVEN, NEITHER DRAWABLE: physics/livePanel.js AND physics/viewLayout.js NOW HAVE A PAGE. *** v3451's instruction has been READ THEM ONE AT A TIME for eleven rounds and this is one of them read properly. THE FIRST THING MEASURED KILLED THE EASY ANSWER: v3673 had just established that a tools.html row IS a door, so the obvious move was to give the seven undecided modules rows -- AND NOT ONE OF THE SEVEN HAS A MAIN BLOCK. A row runs a file BY PATH and a file with no main block prints nothing, so a row would have been a button that does nothing, and the count would have fallen for a reason that was not true. ADDING DOORS TO MAKE A NUMBER FALL IS THE SAME DEFECT AS RECLASSIFYING TO MAKE IT FALL, one round later and wearing the previous round's fix as a costume. *** *** WHAT THEY ACTUALLY ARE: ONE FEATURE SPLIT ACROSS TWO FILES, AND THE JOIN IS IN THE CODE RATHER THAN IN A COMMENT. viewLayout.validate() defaults its known-panel list to ["spectrum", "zeta-line"] -- WHICH ARE EXACTLY livePanel'S TWO PANELS -- so the layout was written to hold these insets and the insets were written to fill that layout, and the only importer either one has ever had is its own selfcheck. predictions.html describes the page in prose, as a PREDICTION, and nobody built it. THE ARITHMETIC WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM: viewLayout-selfcheck already grades the spectrum peak against the chain's ANALYTIC mode frequency and the zeta dips against the first nontrivial zero. WHAT WAS MISSING WAS THE DOOR, which is the v3617 / v3630 / v3669 shape a fourth time -- except those three were STRUCTURALLY unable to have one (node:url at module scope) and these two were browser-safe all along, so nothing blocked this except nobody doing it. WALKED THE GRAPH RATHER THAN ASSUMING: livePanel -> fft, vibrations, zetaCritical -> strictTrig, strictLog, ZERO node: specifiers anywhere in it. *** NEW live-panel.html, and it DRAWS ITS OWN VERIFICATION rather than asserting it (v3625's idiom): the spectrum's peak bin is converted back to a frequency and tabled beside the analytic one, worst gap 0.34 OF A BIN across modes 3/5/8; the zeta curve is drawn with the PUBLISHED zeros as ticks so the eye checks the dips land ON them. THE ANCHOR IS FROM OUTSIDE THIS ENGINE -- 14.134725, 21.022040, 25.010858 -- which is what makes it a key rather than a mirror, since nothing in this tree computes them and an internal check could only show the panel agreeing with itself. *** *** AND THE PAGE TELLS YOU NOT TO READ ITS OWN BEST-LOOKING COLUMN AS PRECISION. The dips come out at 14.100, 21.000 and 25.000, and the gaps to the published values look like 0.035 / 0.022 / 0.011 -- BUT A DIP IS THE INDEX OF A LOCAL MINIMUM ON A SAMPLED CURVE, and the default sweep is 190 steps across t = 8..27, A GRID SPACING OF 0.1. Those three numbers are grid points. THE GAP COLUMN IS BOUNDED BY THE SAMPLING RATE AND NOT BY ANYTHING THE PANEL GOT RIGHT, so quoting it as accuracy would be reporting the step size wearing a measurement's clothes -- v3619's law (an observable that is an index cannot see a shift smaller than one cell) arriving on a page instead of in a gate. Raise steps and the gaps shrink WITH THE GRID, which is the tell. *** graveyard's actionable falls 88 -> 86 BY WIRING RATHER THAN BY RECLASSIFYING, and the baseline is re-derived in the same edit -- with the distinction written into the source, because a baseline that fell because a round PAID debt must not be confused with one that fell because a round NARROWED what counts as debt (v3673). SHOWN FAILING: delete the viewLayout import from the page -- half a door -- and the count goes 86 -> 87 and the ratchet red, tree restored BYTE-IDENTICAL. *** NOT CLAIMED: that the page LOOKS right. It runs 8192 samples on load and blocks for as long as that takes, which it says out loud; I cannot see a canvas. FIVE OF THE SEVEN REMAIN UNDECIDED and are deliberately untouched -- coverage, routeContrast, skillTrial, suspiciousZero and trendVsNoise are libraries whose only caller is a gate, and whether that is legitimate is a judgement per module, not a page. CARRIED REDS: gateQuality 2 (diffed against pristine, not mine), graveyard 1 (the total, left red on v3673's stated grounds). // v3673 -- *** SIXTEEN OF GRAVEYARD'S HUNDRED-AND-FOUR "ORPHANED UTILITIES" ALREADY HAD A FRONT DOOR, AND TWO OF THEM ARE THE PAIR A ROUND BUILT A DOOR FOR ON PURPOSE. *** graveyard's own failure line says of every member "these export functions and NOTHING calls them -- wire it, or delete it", and its resolver is THE IMPORT GRAPH. A reportingTools row does not import its module; it SPAWNS IT BY PATH from a button on tools.html. THAT IS v3608's FINDING ONE REGISTER OVER -- the front-door law has one shape of door and the tree has four -- and the proof is buildKnowledgeIndex.mjs and buildPageIndex.mjs, WHICH ARE EXACTLY THE PAIR v3609 GAVE ROWS TO IN ORDER TO CLOSE THEIR PROSE DOORS. A round fixed a door and this register never saw the fix, for sixty-four versions. Actionable falls 104 -> 88 WITHOUT A SINGLE MODULE BEING WIRED OR DELETED, and that half of the ratchet is GREEN. *** THE RULE LIVES IN doorKinds.mjs, v3608's OWN MODULE, so this is a continuation rather than a new register: hasToolDoor(rel) READS THE REGISTRY. That is what makes it a NARROWING OF WHAT COUNTS AS DEBT RATHER THAN A LOOSENING OF THE RULE -- delete a row and its module RETURNS TO THE PILE on the next run, which a hand-written exemption list could never do, and which is SHOWN: removing buildPageIndex's row takes doored 16 -> 14, actionable 88 -> 90, and the ratchet RED. The sixteen are PRINTED BY NAME, so nothing leaves the report, only the count. *** *** AND THE TREE STOPPED ME MID-ROUND, WHICH IS THE BEST THING IN IT. I first put the door-aware `continue` INSIDE orphanTriage.actionable(), and orphanTriage-selfcheck went red with a sentence written for exactly this: "this round moves DISPLAY CATEGORISATION only ... CHANGING A CLASSIFIER TO MAKE A COUNT FALL IS HOW A RATCHET STOPS MEANING ANYTHING (v3453)." That is a fair description of the edit I had just made, HOWEVER GOOD THE REASON FELT. REVERTED. The door rule belongs in THE RATCHET THAT MAKES THE CLAIM and not in the classifier that feeds it. *** THE TWO NUMBERS NOW DIFFER ON PURPOSE AND IT IS NOT TWO DECLARATIONS: orphanTriage answers "gate-only BY IMPORT", which the doored sixteen still are, and graveyard answers "ACTIONABLE DEBT", which they are not. Both read the SAME hasToolDoor; only the questions differ, and graveyard prints the sixteen so the gap is visible rather than inferred. *** THE BASELINE IS RE-DERIVED IN THE SAME EDIT, 100 -> 88, AND THAT IS v3672'S LESSON ARRIVING ONE ROUND LATER: leaving it at 100 would have put TWELVE SLOTS OF SLACK in a ratchet, so twelve genuinely orphaned utilities could arrive and be absorbed in silence -- the identical defect v3672 found in pageReach's baseline and had to fix there. WHENEVER A RATCHET'S SET NARROWS, ITS COUNT IS RE-DERIVED IN THE SAME EDIT. *** *** THE OTHER HALF IS DELIBERATELY LEFT RED AND THE BASELINE IS NOT RAISED: the total reads 145 against 117 and the doored sixteen are STILL gate-only by import, so narrowing the actionable count does not and should not move it. Raising 117 to meet 145 is THE ONE MOVE THIS RATCHET EXISTS TO PREVENT -- every analysis primitive the project adds lands here as +1 because it is gate-only AND named in a changelog, so a baseline that rises to meet its reading has stopped measuring. The real fix is the distinction the count cannot draw (named ONLY by a changelog versus named by something that might load it) and that is its own round, not a number typed here. *** AND A THIRD CARRIED RED WAS FOUND BY RUNNING SOMETHING I HAD NOT BEEN RUNNING BY HAND: orphanTriage-selfcheck WAS ALREADY FAILING at v3672 -- PROVEN BY RUNNING IT AGAINST A PRISTINE v3672 EXTRACT rather than assumed -- and I had been reporting the carried reds as two. It compared a FROZEN v3558 reading (before.actionable = 105) against the LIVE count, so it went red the day the real number moved to 104 and stayed red FOR THE WRONG REASON: A REMEMBERED RESULT STANDING IN FOR A CURRENT MEASUREMENT, this tree's most-named shape, inside the file whose subject is derived evidence. Rewritten to the PROPERTY IT WAS REALLY PROTECTING -- the classifier sorts the actionable set and never SHRINKS it -- with BOTH SIDES RE-DERIVED LIVE, so it holds whatever the count becomes. IT IS THE LINE THAT CAUGHT ME EARLIER IN THIS SAME ROUND, which is the argument for keeping it rather than deleting it. NOT CLAIMED: that the 88 are all real debt. Seven of them carry no disposition anywhere in the tree and v3451's instruction still stands -- READ THEM ONE AT A TIME. CARRIED REDS NOW: gateQuality 2, graveyard 1 (the total, explained above). // v3672 -- *** THE OLDEST STANDING RED IS GREEN: pageReach HAS BEEN NAMING raycast.html, box3d-contacts.html AND instrument-bench.html AS INVISIBLE FOR DOZENS OF ROUNDS AND I HAVE BEEN REPORTING IT AS "PRE-EXISTING" EVERY SHIP. *** Keith asked whether it had been done. It had not. The gate's own words offer two ways out -- "LINK THEM, or add them to pageReach-baseline.json WITH A REASON" -- AND THE SECOND ONE IS NOT AVAILABLE: the baseline's note says it MAY SHRINK AND MAY NEVER GROW, and the gate asserts that sentence is still in the file. A LIST THAT MAY